{"level":30,"time":1777030656108,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DE6Gf7jqYNcmb5DjcMrYVs","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 9 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: drawing — Notes detailing lighting requirements, specifically that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. It also includes illustrations of fully-shielded light fixtures.\n     4→\n     5→Lighting Note\n     6→All exterior lighting will be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. All site lighting to be located on the building will be in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5, and will be reviewed during building plan review. Any change or substitution of lams/light fixtures shall be submitted to the Director for approval in accordance with Section 2.5.2.E. See Figure 34.\n     7→[Illustration of four different fully-shielded light fixtures]\n     8→Figure 34\n     9→Examples of Fully-Shielded Light Fixtures\n    10→\n    11→## Block 2: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan for the Valley View Townhomes project. It shows the layout of multiple buildings, driveways, sidewalks, parking spaces, detention pond, retaining walls, utility lines, and easements. Key features include buildings numbered 1 through 8, various unit numbers, concrete sidewalks, decomposed granite walkways, a mail kiosk, and existing structures to be replaced or retained.\n    12→\n    13→Site Plan showing layout of buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8), driveways, parking, detention pond, utility lines, easements, existing trees, retaining walls, and surrounding properties.\n    14→\n    15→## Block 3: notes — A note specifying the required methods for installing fence post holes near a tree's Critical Root Zone (CRZ) to avoid damage, including manual digging and the application of hardwood mulch.\n    16→\n    17→Fence Note\n    18→Post holes that are to be installed near a trees Critical Root Zone (CRZ) will be dug by hand. No machinery is allowed in the CRZ and any work done in this area shall be done by hand. Add a 3-inch layer of hardwood mulch in the CRZ prior to commencing construction activities.\n    19→\n    20→## Block 4: notes — Notes regarding accessibility standards and requirements for the project, covering topics such as accessible parking, route cross-slopes, and ramp design.\n    21→\n    22→Accessibility Notes\n    23→1. Approval of these plans by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only. Compliance with accessibility standards such as the 2010 Standards for Accessible Design or the 2012 Texas Accessibility Standards was not verified. The applicant is responsible for compliance with all applicable accessibility standards.\n    24→2. Accessible parking spaces must be located on a surface with a slope not exceeding 1:50.\n    25→3. Accessible routes must have a cross-slope no greater than 1:50.\n    26→4. Slopes on accessible routes may not exceed 1:20 unless designed as a ramp.\n    27→5. The maximum slope of a ramp in new construction is 1:12. The maximum rise for any ramp is 20in. The maximum horizontal projection is 30 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:12 and 1:15, and 40 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:16 and 1:20.\n    28→\n    29→## Block 5: notes — A list of notes related to the release of the site plan, covering topics such as compliance with the approved plan, necessary permits, signage, easements, utility service, demolition, condominium regulations, driveway construction, and right-of-way excavation.\n    30→\n    31→a. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require site plan amendment and approval of the Development Services Department.\n    32→b. Approval of this Site Plan does not include Building and Fire Code approval nor building permit approval.\n    33→c. All signs must comply with requirements of the Land Development Code (Chapter 25-10).\n    34→d. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n    35→e. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n    36→f. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n    37→g. No certificate of occupancy may be issued for the proposed residential condominium project until the owner or owners of the property have complied with Chapter 81 and 82 of the Property Code of the State of Texas or any other statutes enacted by the State concerning condominiums.\n    38→h. For driveway construction: The owner is responsible for all costs for relocation of, or damage to utilities.\n    39→i. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n    40→\n    41→## Block 6: notes — General notes outlining the engineer's responsibilities, the limitations of the provided utility locations, and the contractor's obligation to verify these locations prior to construction.\n    42→\n    43→Release of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n    44→The location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n    45→\n    46→## Block 7: notes — Notes detailing regulations for trash collection on the property, including rules against leaving containers at the curbside unattended and the requirement for visible signage.\n    47→\n    48→Trash Collection Notes\n    49→Trash containers shall not be left at curb side unattended. Signs must be posted on the building or at a location that is publicly visible indicating that trash containers may not be left at curbside. The sign shall indicate the City of Austin's Code enforcement phone number for the public to report violations.\n    50→A restrictive covenant may be required for enforcement purposes.\n    51→Reference: TCM, 9.4.2\n    52→\n    53→## Block 8: notes — Information about two benchmarks used for the project, including their location (Mag Nail Set in Asphalt) and elevation in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n    54→\n    55→Benchmarks\n    56→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    57→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    58→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    59→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    60→\n    61→## Block 9: notes — The legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    62→\n    63→Legal Description\n    64→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    65→\n    66→## Block 10: notes — A table detailing the parking requirements and provisions for the project, including land use, parking spaces provided (surface/garage), compact spaces, ADA spaces, and bicycle spaces.\n    67→\n    68→Parking Data\n    69→Land Use Residential\n    70→Parking Spaces Provided 10 Surface/ 78 Garage\n    71→Compact Spaces Provided 0\n    72→ADA Spaces Required X\n    73→ADA Spaces Provided X\n    74→Total Bicycle Spaces Required 5\n    75→Long Term Bicycle Spaces Required 3\n    76→Long Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 39 (1 per Garage)\n    77→Short Term Bicycle Spaces Required 2\n    78→Short Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 2\n    79→\n    80→## Block 11: other — A graphic scale indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet on the site plan.\n    81→\n    82→0 20' 40'\n    83→Scale 1\" = 20'\n    84→\n    85→## Block 14: table — This table provides data on building heights, including the number of units, building type, average mean sea level (MSL), building height in feet, average building height (MSL), height allowed in feet, and height allowed (MSL).\n    86→\n    87→| Building # | Building Type | Average (MSL) | Building Height (FT) | Avg. Building Height (MSL) | Height Allowed (FT) | Height Allowed (MSL) |\n    88→| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n    89→| 1 | 4 Plex | 686.25 | 35' - 10.75\" | 722.15 | 40' | 726.25 |\n    90→| 2 | 6 Plex | 684.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.90 | 40' | 724.50 |\n    91→| 3 | 5 Plex | 685.00 | 36' - 1\" | 721.08 | 40' | 725.00 |\n    92→| 4 | 5 Plex | 684.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 724.25 |\n    93→| 5 | 4 Plex | 682.17 | 35' - 10.75\" | 718.07 | 40' | 722.17 |\n    94→| 6 | 4 Plex | 681.75 | 35' - 10.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 721.75 |\n    95→| 7 | 5 Plex | 682.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.90 | 40' | 722.50 |\n    96→| 8 | 6 Plex | 682.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.65 | 40' | 722.25 |\n    97→\n    98→## Block 15: table — This table provides data for various buildings in the project, including building type (e.g., 4 Plex, 6 Plex), gross floor area (sf), building coverage (sf), bedroom type, and number of units.\n    99→\n   100→| Building # | Building Type | Gross Floor Area (sf) | Building Coverage (sf) | Bedroom Type | Units |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd542ab3-a24e-4d13-af12-e84b414b992a","uuid":"6e732f96-2c44-450f-89cc-a6aacc9a1ce1","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-09/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 9 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: drawing — Notes detailing lighting requirements, specifically that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. It also includes illustrations of fully-shielded light fixtures.\n\nLighting Note\nAll exterior lighting will be full cut-off and fully shielded in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5. All site lighting to be located on the building will be in compliance with Subchapter E 2.5, and will be reviewed during building plan review. Any change or substitution of lams/light fixtures shall be submitted to the Director for approval in accordance with Section 2.5.2.E. See Figure 34.\n[Illustration of four different fully-shielded light fixtures]\nFigure 34\nExamples of Fully-Shielded Light Fixtures\n\n## Block 2: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan for the Valley View Townhomes project. It shows the layout of multiple buildings, driveways, sidewalks, parking spaces, detention pond, retaining walls, utility lines, and easements. Key features include buildings numbered 1 through 8, various unit numbers, concrete sidewalks, decomposed granite walkways, a mail kiosk, and existing structures to be replaced or retained.\n\nSite Plan showing layout of buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8), driveways, parking, detention pond, utility lines, easements, existing trees, retaining walls, and surrounding properties.\n\n## Block 3: notes — A note specifying the required methods for installing fence post holes near a tree's Critical Root Zone (CRZ) to avoid damage, including manual digging and the application of hardwood mulch.\n\nFence Note\nPost holes that are to be installed near a trees Critical Root Zone (CRZ) will be dug by hand. No machinery is allowed in the CRZ and any work done in this area shall be done by hand. Add a 3-inch layer of hardwood mulch in the CRZ prior to commencing construction activities.\n\n## Block 4: notes — Notes regarding accessibility standards and requirements for the project, covering topics such as accessible parking, route cross-slopes, and ramp design.\n\nAccessibility Notes\n1. Approval of these plans by the City of Austin indicates compliance with applicable City regulations only. Compliance with accessibility standards such as the 2010 Standards for Accessible Design or the 2012 Texas Accessibility Standards was not verified. The applicant is responsible for compliance with all applicable accessibility standards.\n2. Accessible parking spaces must be located on a surface with a slope not exceeding 1:50.\n3. Accessible routes must have a cross-slope no greater than 1:50.\n4. Slopes on accessible routes may not exceed 1:20 unless designed as a ramp.\n5. The maximum slope of a ramp in new construction is 1:12. The maximum rise for any ramp is 20in. The maximum horizontal projection is 30 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:12 and 1:15, and 40 feet for a ramp with a slope between 1:16 and 1:20.\n\n## Block 5: notes — A list of notes related to the release of the site plan, covering topics such as compliance with the approved plan, necessary permits, signage, easements, utility service, demolition, condominium regulations, driveway construction, and right-of-way excavation.\n\na. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require site plan amendment and approval of the Development Services Department.\nb. Approval of this Site Plan does not include Building and Fire Code approval nor building permit approval.\nc. All signs must comply with requirements of the Land Development Code (Chapter 25-10).\nd. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\ne. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\nf. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\ng. No certificate of occupancy may be issued for the proposed residential condominium project until the owner or owners of the property have complied with Chapter 81 and 82 of the Property Code of the State of Texas or any other statutes enacted by the State concerning condominiums.\nh. For driveway construction: The owner is responsible for all costs for relocation of, or damage to utilities.\ni. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n\n## Block 6: notes — General notes outlining the engineer's responsibilities, the limitations of the provided utility locations, and the contractor's obligation to verify these locations prior to construction.\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n\n## Block 7: notes — Notes detailing regulations for trash collection on the property, including rules against leaving containers at the curbside unattended and the requirement for visible signage.\n\nTrash Collection Notes\nTrash containers shall not be left at curb side unattended. Signs must be posted on the building or at a location that is publicly visible indicating that trash containers may not be left at curbside. The sign shall indicate the City of Austin's Code enforcement phone number for the public to report violations.\nA restrictive covenant may be required for enforcement purposes.\nReference: TCM, 9.4.2\n\n## Block 8: notes — Information about two benchmarks used for the project, including their location (Mag Nail Set in Asphalt) and elevation in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 9: notes — The legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 10: notes — A table detailing the parking requirements and provisions for the project, including land use, parking spaces provided (surface/garage), compact spaces, ADA spaces, and bicycle spaces.\n\nParking Data\nLand Use Residential\nParking Spaces Provided 10 Surface/ 78 Garage\nCompact Spaces Provided 0\nADA Spaces Required X\nADA Spaces Provided X\nTotal Bicycle Spaces Required 5\nLong Term Bicycle Spaces Required 3\nLong Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 39 (1 per Garage)\nShort Term Bicycle Spaces Required 2\nShort Term Bicycle Spaces Provided 2\n\n## Block 11: other — A graphic scale indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet on the site plan.\n\n0 20' 40'\nScale 1\" = 20'\n\n## Block 14: table — This table provides data on building heights, including the number of units, building type, average mean sea level (MSL), building height in feet, average building height (MSL), height allowed in feet, and height allowed (MSL).\n\n| Building # | Building Type | Average (MSL) | Building Height (FT) | Avg. Building Height (MSL) | Height Allowed (FT) | Height Allowed (MSL) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | 4 Plex | 686.25 | 35' - 10.75\" | 722.15 | 40' | 726.25 |\n| 2 | 6 Plex | 684.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.90 | 40' | 724.50 |\n| 3 | 5 Plex | 685.00 | 36' - 1\" | 721.08 | 40' | 725.00 |\n| 4 | 5 Plex | 684.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 724.25 |\n| 5 | 4 Plex | 682.17 | 35' - 10.75\" | 718.07 | 40' | 722.17 |\n| 6 | 4 Plex | 681.75 | 35' - 10.75\" | 717.65 | 40' | 721.75 |\n| 7 | 5 Plex | 682.50 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.90 | 40' | 722.50 |\n| 8 | 6 Plex | 682.25 | 33' - 4.75\" | 715.65 | 40' | 722.25 |\n\n## Block 15: table — This table provides data for various buildings in the project, including building type (e.g., 4 Plex, 6 Plex), gross floor area (sf), building coverage (sf), bedroom type, and number of units.\n\n| Building # | Building Type | Gross Floor Area (sf) | Building Coverage (sf) | Bedroom Type | Units |","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":139}}}
