{"level":30,"time":1777030984915,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_0126axdJcsRTyKcwoKSpE9hX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, I should verify my findings more thoroughly before submitting. Let me reconsider whether I've checked all 15 checklist items. Looking at the checklist items again:\n\nEL-2.1 - Non-utility-compatible trees within 15 ft of distribution - NOT-VERIFIABLE (species not identified)\nEL-2.2 - Non-utility-compatible trees within 50 ft of transmission - N/A or NOT-VERIFIABLE (no transmission lines identified, only distribution)\nEL-2.3 - Large trees within 25 ft of distribution - NOT-VERIFIABLE (species not identified)\nEL-2.4 - Large trees within 50 ft of transmission - N/A (no transmission lines)\nEL-2.5 - Trees in transmission easement - N/A (no transmission easement, facts.md shows 5' electric easements not transmission)\nEL-2.6 - Trees within 10 ft of poles - NOT-VERIFIABLE (poles not clearly marked, distances not shown)\nEL-2.7 - Trees within 10 ft of pad-mounted equipment - NOT-VERIFIABLE (pad locations shown on Sheet 21 but not cross-referenced with landscape)\nEL-2.8 - Trees within 5 ft of underground equipment - NOT-VERIFIABLE (underground pull boxes shown on Sheet 21 but not cross-referenced)\nEL-2.9 - Trees within 20 ft of underground equipment lack root barriers - NOT-VERIFIABLE (no root barriers shown, distances not verifiable)\nEL-2.10 - Plant legend doesn't identify species - FAIL (confirmed)\nEL-2.11 - Large trees not differentiated - NOT-VERIFIABLE (can't identify large trees on plan)\nEL-2.12 - Clearances measured from pole centerline instead of conductor - NOT-VERIFIABLE (no dimensions shown at all)\n\n\nEL-2.13 - Clearances measured from canopy instead of trunk - NOT-VERIFIABLE (no dimensions shown)\nEL-2.14 - Trees not shown in relation to OHE - FAIL (no dimension or call-out showing relationship)\nEL-2.15 - No Austin Energy approval for exceptions - NOT-VERIFIABLE or N/A depending on interpretation (no exceptions claimed)\n\nNow I need to be more systematic about this. The key question is whether transmission infrastructure exists at all. Looking at facts.md and the electrical plan, there's no mention of transmission easements—only 5' electric easements for distribution. This means several items are automatically N/A rather than NOT-VERIFIABLE.\n\nFor utility poles, the small circular symbols on Sheet 21 where underground lines connect might indicate pole locations, but they're not explicitly labeled, making any clearance measurements unreliable. The pad-mounted transformers shown on Sheet 21 have no corresponding distances to trees on the landscape plan. Underground electric lines need to be evaluated separately.\n\nReconsidering EL-2.15, the requirement only applies when trees are proposed within prohibited clearance zones without documented Austin Energy approval. The vision analysis suggests trees exist within 15 feet of the overhead line along the southern boundary, which would trigger this requirement. However, I can't determine from the available plans whether documented approval exists or not, so this should be marked NOT-VERIFIABLE rather than a clear failure.\n\nFor the other items, I need to be more precise about what can actually be verified. EL-2.1 and EL-2.3 are NOT-VERIFIABLE since species aren't identified—though trees are visibly present near the OHE line, I can't confirm whether they're utility-compatible without knowing what they are. EL-2.6 through EL-2.9 are also NOT-VERIFIABLE because electrical equipment and tree locations appear on different sheets without clear cross-referencing. EL-2.10 is a clear FAIL since species identification is missing from the landscape plan entirely. EL-2.11 cannot be verified without species data to determine which trees qualify as large. EL-2.12 and EL-2.13 follow similar patterns of insufficient information to make definitive determinations.\n\nEL-2.14 presents trees and OHE together but lacks documented clearance specifications, making it a FAIL. EL-2.15 is NOT-VERIFIABLE since there's no evidence of Austin Energy approval either way.\n\nI should also reconsider whether EL-2.2, EL-2.4, and EL-2.5 are actually N/A. The regulatory guidance notes that most overhead facilities are distribution unless explicitly marked as transmission or within transmission easements, with transmission being physically larger and having dedicated easements. The site shows two dedicated 5-foot electric easements but no transmission easements mentioned, suggesting these are distribution lines.\n\nLooking at potential PASS items: EL-2.5 could pass since there's no transmission easement present, though N/A is more appropriate. Items EL-2.6 through EL-2.9 can't pass without verifiable distances, and EL-2.12 through EL-2.13 lack the required dimensions. For EL-2.11, which applies only when large trees (40+ feet) are proposed near overhead facilities, the plant schedule shows Live Oak with a 16-foot minimum height, which doesn't meet the large tree threshold. of the proposed species?\n\nLive Oak and Cedar Elm likely meet the 40-foot threshold based on typical mature heights, making EL-2.11 potentially applicable. The plant schedule lists container heights rather than mature landscape heights—Live Oak and Cedar Elm show 16' minimum as container specimens, which doesn't address the mature height requirement from ECM Appendix F. Texas Mountain Laurel and Mexican Redbud appear to be smaller ornamentals that wouldn't trigger this criterion.\n\nThe core issue is that large trees aren't differentiated on the landscape plan itself. Without mature height notations or some visual distinction marking which tree symbols represent large specimens, there's no way to verify clearances from overhead electric facilities. This makes the requirement not verifiable—the plan doesn't provide the necessary information to confirm compliance with clearance distances.\n\nHowever, applying the grouping's criteria more strictly: the required element (large tree designation with mature heights) is completely absent from the plans. Since the instructions specify marking as FAIL when required information is missing, EL-2.11 should be marked as FAIL rather than NOT-VERIFIABLE. The same logic applies to EL-2.10—species identification is absent from the legend, so that's also a FAIL. For EL-2.14, I need to examine whether trees are shown in relation to overhead electric facilities and whether that relationship is clearly documented on the plan.\n\nFor EL-2.14, the trees and OHE line both appear on the plan, but the clearance relationship between them isn't adequately documented. Without dimension lines or clear spatial notation showing how the trees relate to the overhead facilities, the requirement to show them \"in relation to\" each other isn't satisfied. This missing relational documentation qualifies as a FAIL condition. I should also revisit the NOT-VERIFIABLE designations for EL-2.1 and EL-2.3, since the core deficiency—missing species identification—prevents verification of whether non-utility-compatible trees are within the specified lateral distances. EL-2.13 focuses on how clearances are measured—specifically whether they're taken from the tree canopy versus the trunk. The applicability condition states these items only apply \"when tree clearances are dimensioned on plans.\" Since the landscape plan shows no dimension lines or specific clearance measurements between the proposed trees and the OHE line, both EL-2.12 and EL-2.13 should be marked N/A rather than NOT-VERIFIABLE.\n\nThis means my revised compliance list should categorize EL-2.2, EL-2.4, EL-2.5, EL-2.12, and EL-2.13 as not applicable—EL-2.2 through EL-2.5 due to the absence of transmission lines or easements, and EL-2.12 and EL-2.13 because tree clearances aren't dimensioned on the plans.\n\nThe failures are EL-2.10, EL-2.11, and EL-2.14, where species identification is missing from the landscape plan, large trees aren't differentiated, and trees aren't shown in relation to overhead equipment with proper clearance documentation. Several items can't be verified: EL-2.1 and EL-2.3 lack species identification needed to confirm utility compatibility and large tree status; EL-2.6 through EL-2.9 are missing distance measurements and details for poles, pads, underground equipment, and root barriers; EL-2.15 has no supporting evidence.\n\nFor EL-2.15 specifically, the requirement only applies when trees occupy prohibited clearance zones without documented Austin Energy approval. Since the landscape plan shows trees positioned close to the overhead line—likely within the 15-foot prohibited zone—but species are unidentified, I can't determine if they're utility-compatible or prohibited. Without confirmation of species status or Austin Energy approval documentation, this item remains unverifiable.\n\nI need to reconsider my submission. I flagged four deficiencies (EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.10, and EL-2.14), but the schema requires including all items marked as fail or not-verifiable. Many standards share the same verification challenge—insufficient documentation prevents confirmation either way. I should expand my findings to capture all items that genuinely cannot be verified rather than limiting to just a few.\n\nLooking at each standard individually: EL-2.1 fails verification because trees near overhead electrical equipment lack species identification. EL-2.3 has the same issue—I can't determine if large trees are properly positioned without knowing what species they are. EL-2.6 presents another problem: utility poles appear inferred rather than explicitly marked on the landscape plan, making distance measurements impossible to confirm. EL-2.7 similarly lacks cross-reference between equipment pads shown on one sheet and their relationship to landscaping elements.\n\nThe same verification gap applies to EL-2.8 through EL-2.9—underground infrastructure and pull boxes aren't coordinated with the landscape plan, so I can't measure clearances. EL-2.11 fails because the landscape plan doesn't designate which trees qualify as \"large.\" EL-2.15 shows trees potentially in restricted zones, but without documented approval or clear tree specifications, I can't confirm compliance with both conditions.\n\nI need to reconsider EL-2.6 more carefully though. The standard requires overhead poles to be present or proposed, and I should verify whether poles actually appear on the landscape plan rather than assuming they're just inferred. should be NOT-VERIFIABLE since poles can't be reliably located from the plans.\n\nLooking at the broader picture, the core issue is that trees are shown on the landscape plan without species identification, while electrical infrastructure appears on a separate sheet with no cross-reference between the two. There are also no dimension lines indicating clearances. This lack of coordination between plans and missing species data means I can't verify several checklist items—specifically EL-2.1 due to unidentified species, and EL-2.3 along with others that depend on measuring distances to electrical components that aren't clearly marked.\n\nThe items that should definitely fail are EL-2.10 (species identification is explicitly required but absent) and EL-2.14 (trees and overhead electrical equipment aren't documented with clearance relationships). EL-2.11 might also be a failure if large trees should be differentiated but aren't. The remaining items fall into NOT-VERIFIABLE because the necessary cross-references and measurements don't exist on the plans to confirm compliance.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2479,"cache_read_input_tokens":62959,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input