 the more and southwest, subtropical south, and temperate east. In the event an isolated and rare occurrence is identified, the tree should be treated as native and protected.\n\nAll survey size non-native, invasive woody plants, as identified by the City of Austin, are required to be shown on the tree survey though no mitigation will be required. These trees include, but may not be limited to *Atlanthus altissima*, Tree of heaven, *Albizia julibrissin*, Mimosa, *Broussonetia papyrifera*, Paper mulberry, *Morus alba*, White mulberry, *Elaeagnus angustifolia*, Russian olive, *Firmiana simplex*, Chinese parasol, *Koelreuteria paniculata*, Golden ram, *Ligustrum spp.*, Privet, *Melta aszedarach*, Chinaberry, *Nandina domestica*, Nandina, *Photinia spp.*, Photinia, *Pisstacia chinensis*, Chinese pistache, *Pyracantha coccinea*, Pyracantha, *Tamarix spp.*, Salt cedar, *Triadica sehifera*, Chinese tallow, *Ulmus pumila*, Siberian elm, *Vitex agnus-castus*, Lilac chaste.\n\n*Source: Rule No. [R161-21.16](), 6-17-2021.*\n","numLines":115,"startLine":1,"totalLines":115}}}
