{"level":30,"time":1777031854460,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014mzb6YBLxH9QdC277GsTJT","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# City Arborist Review\n     2→\n     3→*Specifies tree preservation criteria, variance requirements for heritage trees, and mitigation procedures.*\n     4→\n     5→City Arborist Review – Alexander DeRoehn (512) 974-6597 - alexander.deroehn@austintexas.gov\n     6→\n     7→Update 0 6/13/25\n     8→Update 1 9/26/25\n     9→Note: Please e-mail me if you have any questions, concerns, or require additional information about these comments: Alexander.Deroehn@austintexas.gov. You may also e-mail me if you would like to schedule a phone or virtual meeting to clarify the review comments. Updates cannot be reviewed outside of the update review cycle.\n    10→\n    11→CA1 – Current Status: Pending\n    12→* U1: Remove plantings from the half CRZ of trees to remain.\n    13→* U0: Ensure all trees meet the following tree preservation criteria: \n    14→  1. At least fifty percent of the total area (square footage) of the Critical Root Zone must be preserved at natural grade, with natural ground cover. \n    15→  2. The entirety of the Half CRZ must be protected, with the exception that cut or fill of 4\" or less is allowed within the Half CRZ. \n    16→  3. No cut or fill is allowed within the Quarter CRZ. \n    17→Reference: ECM 3.5.2.A.1\n    18→\n    19→[Figure: Series of four circular diagrams illustrating allowable impacts to the Critical Root Zone (CRZ) of a tree, showing 30%, 60%, 50%, and 75% impacts respectively]\n    20→\n    21→CA2 – Current Status: Pending\n    22→* U1: Not addressed. A heritage American elm is shown to be removed on landscape sheets. \n    23→* U0: Administrative Variance\n    24→An administrative variance may be granted for a heritage tree that does not have at least one stem that is 30 inches in diameter or larger to be removed only after determining by the City Arborist that the tree is dead, diseased, or an imminent hazard. If not dead, diseased or an imminent hazard, clearly show that the tree prevents reasonable use or access of the property and that all design options have been exhausted.\n    25→Reference: LDC Section 25-8-642\n    26→\n    27→The following items are required to review an administrative variance request to remove a Heritage tree:\n    28→1. Please provide a variance request memo on letterhead (any letterhead works) that states tree id, species and DBH of heritage tree(s) proposed for removal. Reference LDC 25-8-642 to prepare the memo.\n    29→2. Letter shall include applicant findings per the LDC and provide alternative layouts demonstrating preservation is not feasible. \n    30→3. Please pay the administrative variance fee. This fee is added once the memo has been received. \n    31→4. If removal is condition related, provide a third-party arborist report with clear photos of the tree’s condition. Please provide a ISA basic tree risk assessment report if trying to claim DDI status. \n    32→5. If a variance, waiver, exemption, modification, or alternative compliance from another City Code provision would eliminate the need for a variance from LDC Section 25-8-641 before requesting a variance to allow removal of a heritage tree. Please demonstrate that a variance, waiver, exemption, modification, or alternative compliance from another City Code provision that would eliminate the need for a variance has been denied. \n    33→\n    34→CA3 – Current Status: Pending\n    35→* U1: Not addressed. Show this on all sheets including demolition erosion and site plan sheets. \n    36→* U0: Graphically show the Half critical root zone of all trees located adjacent to proposed demolition and construction for review. \n    37→\n    38→CA4 – Current Status: Pending\n    39→* U1: Not addressed. Tree fencing is missing from the following trees: Entire east property line, 4038 and 4039, 4018 and 4019. Fencing is shown for tree 4045 despite this being shown as removed. \n    40→* U0: Expand tree fencing to the furthest extent possible for each tree, ensuring compliance with CA1. Tree fencing cannot be placed over existing, or proposed hardscape, grading etc. Provide fencing for all trees with a CRZ extending into the LOC. Show fencing on erosion control and demolition sheets. \n    41→\n    42→CA5 – Current Status: Pending\n    43→* U1: Silt fence is still shown within the CRZ of trees to be retained. \n    44→* U0: ESC Plan\n    45→Where it is to be installed within the critical root zones of regulated size trees, within and adjacent to the limits of construction, remove silt fence and replace with mulch socks or triangular sedimentation filter systems (silt fence requires 6” of cut for installation). Show the mulch socks or triangular sedimentation filter systems with a symbol in place of a silt fencing symbol. \n    46→Erosion and sediment controls should be placed outside of the tree protection fencing so that fencing will not need to be moved to maintain the controls.\n    47→Reference: ECM 3.5.2\n    48→\n    49→CA6 – Current Status: Pending\n    50→* U1: Not addressed. Tree 4022 critical root zones on the erosion control plan are still not shown to scale.. \n    51→* U0: Trees are to be represented on plans by a circle centered on the trunk location, where the radius of the circle is a number of feet equal to the diameter in inches of the tree. Tree CRZs appear to be incorrect size based on the provided survey. \n    52→Reference: ECM 3.3.2.D.2\n    53→\n    54→CA7 – Current Status: Cleared\n    55→* U0: Provide a tree list on sheet 8 listing all trees, sizes species and labeling which trees are to be removed. \n    56→\n    57→CA8 – Current Status: Pending\n    58→* U1: New impacts and construction entrance within the CRZ of trees 4038 and 4039 which appear to exceed 50% allowable impacts (See CA1). Further, new impacts are proposed within the CRZ of trees along the east property line Demonstrate compliance with CA1 and criteria below. \n    59→* U0: Existing Impacts & Meeting 50%\n    60→Proposed impacts in the Half or Quarter CRZ are allowed within the footprint of existing impacts if they do not exceed the depth or width of those impacts. Additional depth of impact may be allowed if the applicant demonstrates the absence of significant roots. \n    61→To determine whether roots are likely to have extended under an existing impact, please contact a private arborist to air-excavate in the critical root zone adjacent to the edge of the impact (slab foundation, asphalt drive, etc)The arborist needs to air-excavate a 1ft width along the entire span of the critical root zone that has been impacted. \n    62→The air-excavation should be at a 24 inch depth. \n    63→The arborist needs to take pictures that clearly show whether or not roots exist along the area of the trench, extending into the impacted area.\n    64→Please provide pictures showing the root system prior to it being backfilled with soil. Ensure that the pictures are well-lit, and the view is not obstructed.\n    65→Reference: ECM 3.5.2.B.4.a\n    66→\n    67→CA9 – Current Status: Pending\n    68→* U1: comment pending\n    69→* U0: Trees 4034, 4036, 4045, 4026, 4014, 4009\n    70→Excessive pruning constitutes removal of a regulated tree per Land Development Code 25-8-603. Pruning shall conform to the most recent ANSI A300 pruning standard. Not more than twenty-five percent of the foliage shall be removed within an annual growing season. Failure to account for the particular branch structure of any regulated tree may result in a plan revision or project delay if approved plans require pruning that would not comply with crown preservation requirements. \n    71→Demonstrate how the proposed construction (e.g. building, grading, detention pond, etc) including the installation of scaffolding, does not require removal of more than 25% of the crown of the preserved trees. Provide a tree crown assessment by a qualified 3rd party arborist that studies the overall proposed impacts to the crown, identifies and shows the exact location of major pruning cuts, and estimates the overall crown percentage to be removed.\n    72→Reference: ECM 3.5.2.A.2\n    73→\n    74→CA10 – Current Status: Pending\n    75→* U1: comment pending\n    76→* U0: Grading\n    77→Modify the proposed grading to meet preservation criteria.\n    78→A grade change of a few inches can be detrimental to a tree, yet most sites require extensive cut and fill in order to manage drainage flow. Some design alternatives which can be used to preserve significant trees are as follows:\n    79→  o Use berms or retaining walls instead of cutting to provide detention.\n    80→  o Provide tree well and/or aeration systems for trees in fill areas (see City of Austin Standard Specifications and City of Austin Standards).\n    81→  o Provide retaining walls to mitigate cuts and fills (see Figure 3-10 in Appendix V of this manual).\n    82→Reference: ECM 3.5.2 .A.3\n    83→\n    84→CA11 – Current Status: Pending\n    85→* U1: comment pending\n    86→* U0: Please demonstrate what the required over-build(s) will be for the proposed building types. This information is necessary to determine where the real line-of-impact will be in relation to the CRZs of trees to be preserved.\n    87→Reference: ECM 3.5.2.A.3\n    88→\n    89→CA12 – Current Status: Pending\n    90→* U1: comment pending\n    91→* U0: Provide a retaining wall detail showing the required over excavation near preserved trees. Ensure that retaining walls, footers and all over excavation is fully outside of the half CRZ. \n    92→\n    93→CA13 – Current Status: Cleared\n    94→* U0: Clarify if wood fence on the east side of the site is existing or proposed. If proposed, provide the following: \n    95→* Relocate entirely outside of the quarter CRZ. \n    96→* For any installation in the Half CRZ \n    97→  o A call out is needed in the corresponding CRZ(s) for: \n    98→    ▪ post holes to be dug by hand or air excavated\n    99→    ▪ post holes shifted to avoid 1.5” diameter or greater roots\n   100→    ▪ a vapor barrier installed between any poured concrete and the soil. \n   101→* Provide a detail in the plan set for the fence posts and footings to be installed near trees.\n   102→\n   103→CA14 – Current Status: Pending\n   104→* U1: There appears to be open areas for planting. Additionally, mitigation trees are shown spaced 20 feet apart. This would likely allow for denser planting. \n   105→* U0: Of the 249.50 mitigation inches owed, only 40 are proposed. Fee in lieu is considered a secondary option to planting. Demonstrate that all plantable space has been utilized before a fee in lieu is discussed. \n   106→\n   107→CA15 – Current Status: Cleared\n   108→* U0: Ensure tree list meets the following\n   109→* Qualifying Species - Significant Shade Providing Trees\n   110→No less than 75% of the total caliper inches of mitigation trees required must be represented by significant shade trees located in the ECM Appendix F. The remaining 25% of total caliper inches may be represented by a mixture of the ECM Appendix F tree species.\n   111→All trees selected must be suitable for the environment of the immediate planting site (see ECM Appendix F for specific categorization such as tree, soil, site, or regulatory qualities). Eligible replacement tree species are listed in Environmental Criteria Manual Appendix F. The City Arborist may approve species not listed in Appendix F for mitigation credit.\n   112→Reference: ECM 3.5.4 2. B.1.b \n   113→* Qualifying Species - Diversity\n   114→  1. When twenty or more inches of replacement trees are planted on a site they must be selected from at least two species.\n   115→  2. When one hundred or more inches of replacement trees are planted on a site they must be selected from at least five species, with no single species representing more than fifty percent of the total caliper of the required mitigation.\n   116→All trees selected must be suitable for the environment of the immediate planting site (see ECM Appendix F for specific categorization such as tree, soil, site, or regulatory qualities).\n   117→Reference: ECM 3.5.4 2. B.1.b\n   118→\n   119→CA16 – Current Status: Pending\n   120→* U1: Comment to remain as reference. Update table with any subsequent plan changes. \n   121→* U0: Once all tree preservation comments have been cleared, please provide the following information on the plan for urban forest accounting purposes:\n   122→\n   123→Surveyed:\n   124→* Total Appendix F tree inches surveyed;\n   125→* Heritage tree inches surveyed;\n   126→* Non-Appendix F tree inches surveyed; (includes invasive and Non-App F)\n   127→* Invasive tree inches surveyed;\n   128→\n   129→Removed:\n   130→* Total Appendix F inches removed;\n   131→* Heritage Tree inches removed;\n   132→* Total Non-Appendix F inches removed; (includes invasive and Non-App F)\n   133→* Invasive inches removed;\n   134→* Total Dead, Diseased, or Imminent Hazard (DDI) inches removed;\n   135→* DDI Appendix F inches removed;\n   136→* DDI Heritage tree inches removed;\n   137→* DDI Non-Appendix F inches removed;\n   138→* DDI Invasive inches removed;\n   139→\n   140→Mitigation:\n   141→* Total mitigation replacement inches planted;\n   142→* Total replacement inches planted on site (private trees);\n   143→* Total replacement ROW inches planted;\n   144→* Private inches owed to Urban Forest Replenishment Fund (UFRF) \n   145→* Public inches owed to UFRF \n   146→* Total non-mitigation inches planted on site; [ECM 3.5.4]\n   147→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88e5e1a0-af42-4247-99ef-1088ed9cc7e1","uuid":"6f07d4c3-89d8-435b-912a-1dfae14399f7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/14-city-arborist-review.md","content":"# City Arborist Review\n\n*Specifies tree preservation criteria, variance requirements for heritage trees, and mitigation procedures.*\n\nCity Arborist Review – Alexander DeRoehn (512) 974-6597 - alexander.deroehn@austintexas.gov\n\nUpdate 0 6/13/25\nUpdate 1 9/26/25\nNote: Please e-mail me if you have any questions, concerns, or require additional information about these comments: Alexander.Deroehn@austintexas.gov. You may also e-mail me if you would like to schedule a phone or virtual meeting to clarify the review comments. Updates cannot be reviewed outside of the update review cycle.\n\nCA1 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Remove plantings from the half CRZ of trees to remain.\n* U0: Ensure all trees meet the following tree preservation criteria: \n  1. At least fifty percent of the total area (square footage) of the Critical Root Zone must be preserved at natural grade, with natural ground cover. \n  2. The entirety of the Half CRZ must be protected, with the exception that cut or fill of 4\" or less is allowed within the Half CRZ. \n  3. No cut or fill is allowed within the Quarter CRZ. \nReference: ECM 3.5.2.A.1\n\n[Figure: Series of four circular diagrams illustrating allowable impacts to the Critical Root Zone (CRZ) of a tree, showing 30%, 60%, 50%, and 75% impacts respectively]\n\nCA2 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Not addressed. A heritage American elm is shown to be removed on landscape sheets. \n* U0: Administrative Variance\nAn administrative variance may be granted for a heritage tree that does not have at least one stem that is 30 inches in diameter or larger to be removed only after determining by the City Arborist that the tree is dead, diseased, or an imminent hazard. If not dead, diseased or an imminent hazard, clearly show that the tree prevents reasonable use or access of the property and that all design options have been exhausted.\nReference: LDC Section 25-8-642\n\nThe following items are required to review an administrative variance request to remove a Heritage tree:\n1. Please provide a variance request memo on letterhead (any letterhead works) that states tree id, species and DBH of heritage tree(s) proposed for removal. Reference LDC 25-8-642 to prepare the memo.\n2. Letter shall include applicant findings per the LDC and provide alternative layouts demonstrating preservation is not feasible. \n3. Please pay the administrative variance fee. This fee is added once the memo has been received. \n4. If removal is condition related, provide a third-party arborist report with clear photos of the tree’s condition. Please provide a ISA basic tree risk assessment report if trying to claim DDI status. \n5. If a variance, waiver, exemption, modification, or alternative compliance from another City Code provision would eliminate the need for a variance from LDC Section 25-8-641 before requesting a variance to allow removal of a heritage tree. Please demonstrate that a variance, waiver, exemption, modification, or alternative compliance from another City Code provision that would eliminate the need for a variance has been denied. \n\nCA3 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Not addressed. Show this on all sheets including demolition erosion and site plan sheets. \n* U0: Graphically show the Half critical root zone of all trees located adjacent to proposed demolition and construction for review. \n\nCA4 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Not addressed. Tree fencing is missing from the following trees: Entire east property line, 4038 and 4039, 4018 and 4019. Fencing is shown for tree 4045 despite this being shown as removed. \n* U0: Expand tree fencing to the furthest extent possible for each tree, ensuring compliance with CA1. Tree fencing cannot be placed over existing, or proposed hardscape, grading etc. Provide fencing for all trees with a CRZ extending into the LOC. Show fencing on erosion control and demolition sheets. \n\nCA5 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Silt fence is still shown within the CRZ of trees to be retained. \n* U0: ESC Plan\nWhere it is to be installed within the critical root zones of regulated size trees, within and adjacent to the limits of construction, remove silt fence and replace with mulch socks or triangular sedimentation filter systems (silt fence requires 6” of cut for installation). Show the mulch socks or triangular sedimentation filter systems with a symbol in place of a silt fencing symbol. \nErosion and sediment controls should be placed outside of the tree protection fencing so that fencing will not need to be moved to maintain the controls.\nReference: ECM 3.5.2\n\nCA6 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Not addressed. Tree 4022 critical root zones on the erosion control plan are still not shown to scale.. \n* U0: Trees are to be represented on plans by a circle centered on the trunk location, where the radius of the circle is a number of feet equal to the diameter in inches of the tree. Tree CRZs appear to be incorrect size based on the provided survey. \nReference: ECM 3.3.2.D.2\n\nCA7 – Current Status: Cleared\n* U0: Provide a tree list on sheet 8 listing all trees, sizes species and labeling which trees are to be removed. \n\nCA8 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: New impacts and construction entrance within the CRZ of trees 4038 and 4039 which appear to exceed 50% allowable impacts (See CA1). Further, new impacts are proposed within the CRZ of trees along the east property line Demonstrate compliance with CA1 and criteria below. \n* U0: Existing Impacts & Meeting 50%\nProposed impacts in the Half or Quarter CRZ are allowed within the footprint of existing impacts if they do not exceed the depth or width of those impacts. Additional depth of impact may be allowed if the applicant demonstrates the absence of significant roots. \nTo determine whether roots are likely to have extended under an existing impact, please contact a private arborist to air-excavate in the critical root zone adjacent to the edge of the impact (slab foundation, asphalt drive, etc)The arborist needs to air-excavate a 1ft width along the entire span of the critical root zone that has been impacted. \nThe air-excavation should be at a 24 inch depth. \nThe arborist needs to take pictures that clearly show whether or not roots exist along the area of the trench, extending into the impacted area.\nPlease provide pictures showing the root system prior to it being backfilled with soil. Ensure that the pictures are well-lit, and the view is not obstructed.\nReference: ECM 3.5.2.B.4.a\n\nCA9 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: comment pending\n* U0: Trees 4034, 4036, 4045, 4026, 4014, 4009\nExcessive pruning constitutes removal of a regulated tree per Land Development Code 25-8-603. Pruning shall conform to the most recent ANSI A300 pruning standard. Not more than twenty-five percent of the foliage shall be removed within an annual growing season. Failure to account for the particular branch structure of any regulated tree may result in a plan revision or project delay if approved plans require pruning that would not comply with crown preservation requirements. \nDemonstrate how the proposed construction (e.g. building, grading, detention pond, etc) including the installation of scaffolding, does not require removal of more than 25% of the crown of the preserved trees. Provide a tree crown assessment by a qualified 3rd party arborist that studies the overall proposed impacts to the crown, identifies and shows the exact location of major pruning cuts, and estimates the overall crown percentage to be removed.\nReference: ECM 3.5.2.A.2\n\nCA10 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: comment pending\n* U0: Grading\nModify the proposed grading to meet preservation criteria.\nA grade change of a few inches can be detrimental to a tree, yet most sites require extensive cut and fill in order to manage drainage flow. Some design alternatives which can be used to preserve significant trees are as follows:\n  o Use berms or retaining walls instead of cutting to provide detention.\n  o Provide tree well and/or aeration systems for trees in fill areas (see City of Austin Standard Specifications and City of Austin Standards).\n  o Provide retaining walls to mitigate cuts and fills (see Figure 3-10 in Appendix V of this manual).\nReference: ECM 3.5.2 .A.3\n\nCA11 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: comment pending\n* U0: Please demonstrate what the required over-build(s) will be for the proposed building types. This information is necessary to determine where the real line-of-impact will be in relation to the CRZs of trees to be preserved.\nReference: ECM 3.5.2.A.3\n\nCA12 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: comment pending\n* U0: Provide a retaining wall detail showing the required over excavation near preserved trees. Ensure that retaining walls, footers and all over excavation is fully outside of the half CRZ. \n\nCA13 – Current Status: Cleared\n* U0: Clarify if wood fence on the east side of the site is existing or proposed. If proposed, provide the following: \n* Relocate entirely outside of the quarter CRZ. \n* For any installation in the Half CRZ \n  o A call out is needed in the corresponding CRZ(s) for: \n    ▪ post holes to be dug by hand or air excavated\n    ▪ post holes shifted to avoid 1.5” diameter or greater roots\n    ▪ a vapor barrier installed between any poured concrete and the soil. \n* Provide a detail in the plan set for the fence posts and footings to be installed near trees.\n\nCA14 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: There appears to be open areas for planting. Additionally, mitigation trees are shown spaced 20 feet apart. This would likely allow for denser planting. \n* U0: Of the 249.50 mitigation inches owed, only 40 are proposed. Fee in lieu is considered a secondary option to planting. Demonstrate that all plantable space has been utilized before a fee in lieu is discussed. \n\nCA15 – Current Status: Cleared\n* U0: Ensure tree list meets the following\n* Qualifying Species - Significant Shade Providing Trees\nNo less than 75% of the total caliper inches of mitigation trees required must be represented by significant shade trees located in the ECM Appendix F. The remaining 25% of total caliper inches may be represented by a mixture of the ECM Appendix F tree species.\nAll trees selected must be suitable for the environment of the immediate planting site (see ECM Appendix F for specific categorization such as tree, soil, site, or regulatory qualities). Eligible replacement tree species are listed in Environmental Criteria Manual Appendix F. The City Arborist may approve species not listed in Appendix F for mitigation credit.\nReference: ECM 3.5.4 2. B.1.b \n* Qualifying Species - Diversity\n  1. When twenty or more inches of replacement trees are planted on a site they must be selected from at least two species.\n  2. When one hundred or more inches of replacement trees are planted on a site they must be selected from at least five species, with no single species representing more than fifty percent of the total caliper of the required mitigation.\nAll trees selected must be suitable for the environment of the immediate planting site (see ECM Appendix F for specific categorization such as tree, soil, site, or regulatory qualities).\nReference: ECM 3.5.4 2. B.1.b\n\nCA16 – Current Status: Pending\n* U1: Comment to remain as reference. Update table with any subsequent plan changes. \n* U0: Once all tree preservation comments have been cleared, please provide the following information on the plan for urban forest accounting purposes:\n\nSurveyed:\n* Total Appendix F tree inches surveyed;\n* Heritage tree inches surveyed;\n* Non-Appendix F tree inches surveyed; (includes invasive and Non-App F)\n* Invasive tree inches surveyed;\n\nRemoved:\n* Total Appendix F inches removed;\n* Heritage Tree inches removed;\n* Total Non-Appendix F inches removed; (includes invasive and Non-App F)\n* Invasive inches removed;\n* Total Dead, Diseased, or Imminent Hazard (DDI) inches removed;\n* DDI Appendix F inches removed;\n* DDI Heritage tree inches removed;\n* DDI Non-Appendix F inches removed;\n* DDI Invasive inches removed;\n\nMitigation:\n* Total mitigation replacement inches planted;\n* Total replacement inches planted on site (private trees);\n* Total replacement ROW inches planted;\n* Private inches owed to Urban Forest Replenishment Fund (UFRF) \n* Public inches owed to UFRF \n* Total non-mitigation inches planted on site; [ECM 3.5.4]\n","numLines":147,"startLine":1,"totalLines":147}}}
{"level":30,"time":1777031854460,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01ADDiQZMovfNA5TQoTNCB9m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The City Arborist Review focuses on tree preservation and mitigation requirements, but doesn't address electrical clearances. However, the Master Comment Report listed UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4 as part of the Austin Energy preliminary design requirements, which is exactly what my grouping covers.\n\nThe fact that there's an Electric Review section in the Master Comment Report but it just lists the code references without detailed comments about clearances suggests the electrical review may not have been completed yet or the specific clearance violations I've identified may not have been caught.\n\nHowever, my job is to conduct a complete review regardless of what comments have been made. I have clear evidence of violations:\n\n1. **Trees within 15 feet of OHE line (EL-2.1, EL-2.3)**:\n   - Westernmost tree: 0 feet from OHE\n   - Central tree: 1.5 feet from OHE  \n   - Easternmost tree: 0 feet from OHE\n\n2. **Trees within 10 feet of utility pole (EL-2.6)**:\n   - Tree at 8.5 feet from pole\n\n3. **Trees within 10 feet of pad-mounted transformer (EL-2.7)**:\n   - Tree at 1.7 feet from transformer pad\n\n4. **No Austin Energy written approval (EL-2.15)** - I found no evidence of this in the plans or comments\n\n5. **No clearance dimensions on landscape plan (EL-2.14)** - The plan shows OHE and trees but no dimensions\n\n6. **Plant legend completeness (EL-2.10)** - The landscape plan shows tree symbols but the specific proposed trees near electrical infrastructure are not labeled with species names on the landscape plan itself - they would need to be cross-referenced to Sheet 35 plant schedule\n\n7. **Unclear if large trees are differentiated (EL-2.11)** - The landscape plan doesn't show which proposed trees are \"large\" (40+ feet mature height)\n   \nFor EL-2.10, the plant schedule on Sheet 35 does identify all species, so that's not technically a violation. The issue is more about the landscape plan itself not clearly showing which symbols correspond to which species near electrical facilities—the connection requires cross-referencing between sheets. EL-2.14 about missing clearance dimensions is the more substantive concern here.\n\nFor EL-2.11, the mature heights are documented on Sheet 35, but again, the landscape plan doesn't visually differentiate which trees will be large at maturity.\n\nThe violations I can confidently identify are: the 8.5-foot distance from the overhead utility pole (EL-2.6), the 1.7-foot distance from pad-mounted equipment (EL-2.7), trees positioned 0-1.5 feet from the OHE line depending on species (EL-2.1 or EL-2.3), and the absence of clearance dimensions on the plan itself (EL-2.14).\n\nFor the trees at 0-1.5 feet from the OHE line, I need to determine whether they violate EL-2.1 or EL-2.3. Cedar Elms are planned at these locations with mature heights of 40-50 feet, which exceeds the 40-foot threshold for large trees under ECM Appendix F.\n\nLooking at the existing tree list on Sheet 32, both Cedar Elm and Live Oak are marked with \"Y\" designation, confirming they're Appendix F species. Since these are large trees (40+ feet mature height) positioned within 15 lateral feet of the overhead electric line, they'd trigger EL-2.3 rather than just EL-2.1. The UC status designation in ECM Appendix F would clarify utility compatibility, but based on their Appendix F classification and proximity to the OHE, the clearance violation appears to apply. with \"x\" in the \"UC\" column of ECM Appendix F, and the grouping identifies specific exceptions like Texas Mountain Laurel and Goldenball Lead-Tree as utility-compatible. A common violation pattern involves species misidentification—when the plant schedule lists trees that lack the \"UC\" designation in ECM Appendix F but are placed in restricted zones. This indicates UC status is definitively documented in ECM Appendix F, which I don't have direct access to, so I can't conclusively verify whether Cedar Elm or Live Oak qualify. That said, I can definitively fail EL-2.3 for the large trees within 25 feet of overhead electric lines, EL-2.6 for the tree within 10 feet of the pole, and EL-2.7 for the tree within 10 feet of the transformer pad.\n\nAdditionally, **EL-2.14** fails because trees lack sufficient clarity and dimensions on the landscape plan to verify clearances, and **EL-2.15** fails due to no evidence of Austin Energy written approval. For **EL-2.1**, I should mark it as not-verifiable since I cannot determine UC status from the documents, though the large trees likely violate this anyway. Regarding **EL-2.12 and EL-2.13**, these measurement methodology issues don't apply here since the landscape plan contains no dimension lines showing clearances at all. However, this connects to the broader EL-2.14 failure.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4541,"cache_read_input_tokens":76918,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4541,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88e5e1a0-af42-4247-99ef-1088ed9cc7e1","uuid":"d4f53eed-2769-41e7-b97b-09f2dd293969"}
