{"level":30,"time":1777031978302,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":5,"runIndex":"run-2","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":11,"prompt":"Examine this grading plan (Sheet 11) and identify:\n1. Spot elevations and grade changes around the site\n2. Drainage flow directions\n3. Any transformer pad locations if visible or marked\n4. Retaining wall locations and heights\n5. Building finished floor elevations and relative grade elevations\n6. Any utilities or infrastructure marked on the grading plan\n7. Topographic contours that show how water would flow, especially near any equipment pads\nAlso note if there's any indication of how surface water drains around buildings and site features.","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a careful and exact visual analysis of the provided grading plan (Sheet 11 for \"Valley View Townhomes\"), here are the findings for each of your requested categories:\n\n**1. Spot elevations and grade changes around the site**\n*   The site generally slopes downward from the West side (entrance area) toward the East/Southeast.\n*   The legend indicates spot elevations are represented by a crosshair `+` symbol, often with two numbers indicating Top of Curb over Bottom of Curb (e.g., `686.50` over `686.00`), or a single number for a flat surface grade.\n*   **West Entrance Area:** High elevations are noted, such as `687.60`, `688.40`, and `686.50/686.00` near the \"Concrete Curb & Gutter\".\n*   **Central Drive Aisles:** Elevations step down gradually. Between Bldg 1 and Bldg 2, elevations are marked around `685.00` to `684.55`. Moving further east between Bldg 3 and Bldg 5, elevations drop to `681.00` and `682.00`.\n*   **South Parking Area:** Elevations continue to drop, with markers showing `681.14`, `680.14`, `679.46`, and dropping to `679.25` near the southwest entrance.\n*   **Eastern Edge:** Elevations along the eastern property line drop significantly, with finished grades near the retaining walls noted in the `679` to `681` range.\n\n**2. Drainage flow directions**\n*   The plan uses small, straight arrows to indicate \"Flow\" (as defined in the Legend).\n*   **General Pattern:** The arrows universally point away from the building foundations, directing runoff into the adjacent paved drive aisles and parking areas.\n*   **Routing:** Once in the drive aisles, the flow arrows direct water generally Eastward and Southward, following the topographic slope toward the indicated \"SD\" (Proposed Storm Drain Line) inlets and the lower retaining wall boundaries.\n*   For example, between Bldg 2 and Bldg 4, arrows point from the buildings into the center aisle, then turn East to flow toward a square storm drain inlet box.\n\n**3. Transformer pad locations**\n*   After a rigorous scan of the entire sheet, **there are no visible labels or markings specifically identifying a \"Transformer Pad\" or \"Transformer\" on this grading plan.**\n*   There are electrical easements noted (e.g., \"Existing 15' Electric Easement\" on the east boundary, \"5' Elec. & Tele. Easement\" on the southeast, and \"3' Elec. Easement\" on the south), but no specific equipment pad footprints are drawn or labeled.\n\n**4. Retaining wall locations and heights**\nA significant retaining wall system surrounds the North, East, and South perimeters of the site.\n*   **North Perimeter:** Labeled **\"Existing Retaining Wall to be Replaced\"**.\n    *   Elevations step down moving west to east. Labels include: `TW 681.30` (Top of Wall) with `BW 680.56` (Bottom of Wall). Further east, it shows `TW 677.25`, and then `TW 671.53` over `BW 671.25`.\n*   **East Perimeter:** Also labeled **\"Existing Retaining Wall to be Replaced\"**.\n    *   Elevations include `TW 681.00`, stepping down to `TW 681.64`, `TW 680.26`, and `TW 679.50` moving south.\n*   **South Perimeter:**\n    *   Starting at the southwest corner, there is a label: **\"Begin Concrete Retaining Wall\"**.\n    *   Moving east along the south line, there are multiple labels for **\"Existing Retaining Wall\"**.\n    *   Elevations show relationship between wall and grade: `FG 682.12 / TW 683.23`, `FG 682.04 / TW 683.50`, `FG 681.44 / TW 683.53`. There is also a note to **\"Tie into existing Retaining Wall Match existing Wall Elevations\"**.\n\n**5. Building finished floor elevations and relative grade elevations**\nThe finished floor elevations (FFE) are clearly marked inside the footprint of each building. In all cases, the FFE is set higher than the immediately surrounding spot elevations to ensure positive drainage away from the structures.\n*   **Bldg. 1:** `FFE = 687.50` (Relative surrounding grades: ~686.00 to 687.00)\n*   **Bldg. 2:** `FFE = 685.50` (Relative surrounding grades: ~684.50 to 685.00)\n*   **Bldg. 3:** `FFE = 685.50` (Relative surrounding grades: ~684.50 to 685.00)\n*   **Bldg. 4:** `FFE = 685.50` (Relative surrounding grades: ~683.50 to 684.50)\n*   **Bldg. 5:** `FFE = 683.00` (Relative surrounding grades: ~681.50 to 682.50)\n*   **Bldg. 6:** `FFE = 683.00` (Relative surrounding grades: ~681.50 to 682.00)\n*   **Bldg. 7:** `FFE = 685.0` (Relative surrounding grades: ~683.50 to 684.50)\n*   **Bldg. 8:** `FFE = 685.50` (Relative surrounding grades: ~684.00 to 685.00)\n\n**6. Any utilities or infrastructure marked on the grading plan**\n*   **Storm Drainage (SD):** Heavy dashed lines labeled \"SD\" (defined in legend as Proposed Storm Drain Line) run extensively through the drive aisles. Numerous square storm drain inlet structures are drawn where these lines intersect or terminate (e.g., between Bldgs 2/4, Bldgs 4/6, and Bldgs 7/8).\n*   **Water/Wastewater:** The far west/northwest corner shows a **\"Wastewater Easement\"**. There are lines indicating an **\"Existing 8\\\" Water Line\"** and markings for **\"8\\\" WL AC\"** near the western entrance.\n*   **Easements:** Various Public Utility Easements are marked with dashed lines around the perimeters, including **\"5' P.U.E.\"** and **\"7.5' P.U.E.\"**. There is also a **\"Sidewalk Easement\"** near the west entrance.\n*   **Accessibility:** Heavily shaded areas indicating \"ADA Ramp\" (per the legend) are present at pedestrian crossings near the site entrances and building walkways.\n\n**7. Topographic contours and surface water drainage**\n*   **Contours:** Solid lines represent \"Proposed Contour\" and dashed lines represent \"Existing Contour\". The proposed contours clearly show a terracing effect, flattening out building pads (which sit on elevated areas) and sloping the pavement areas.\n*   **Drainage Strategy:** There is a clear indication of how surface water drains. The site utilizes a combination of graded slopes (shown by proposed contours pointing downhill toward the east/southeast) and strategically placed storm drain (SD) inlets.\n*   Water is designed to sheet-flow away from the elevated building pads (FFEs) into the paved driveways. The driveways act as conveyance channels, sloped to carry the runoff to the square storm drain inlets located throughout the central and eastern portions of the site, preventing water from pooling against the perimeter retaining walls.","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1423,"outputTokens":1790,"totalTokens":5718,"reasoningTokens":2505},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 2337396]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisually analyze the binary file provided with extreme accuracy. Pay close attention to tables, reproducing them EXACTLY as they appear, character-by-character.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not paraphrase, interpret, or \"fill in\" text. If you cannot read something clearly, state \"text unclear\" rather than guessing. Transcribe field l