{"level":30,"time":1777032041551,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01LGWxKpSVA1PDLEa2xDuHnm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013TYaEkp4jKyikLtXBbFZe5","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"general-purpose","description":"Evaluate all 47 overhead electric clearance checklist items","prompt":"I'm conducting a site plan review for overhead electric clearance requirements (EL-MD-EXP grouping, 47 checklist items total). I have completed research on the Valley View Townhomes project and found the following key facts:\n\n**EXISTING FACILITIES:**\n- Overhead utility line documented on-site along northern property boundary with a utility pole (Sheet 4)\n- Overhead electric line on southern property boundary (Sheets 9, 31)\n- Proposed 8 buildings with various elevations (686.25-685 ft MSL range)\n- Proposed driveways crossing under overhead lines on southern boundary\n- Proposed trees (mitigation trees) near southern overhead line, approximately 10-15 feet away\n- NO building elevations provided to verify vertical clearances\n- NO clearance dimension callouts on any sheets\n- NO surveyed conductor elevations shown\n- NO 48-hour notification note found on cover sheet\n- Discrepancy: Northern overhead utilities on existing survey (Sheet 4) are omitted from proposed site plan (Sheet 9) with no demolition/relocation notes\n\n**DOCUMENTS AVAILABLE:**\n- Sheet 4: Existing Conditions Survey (overhead utilities shown)\n- Sheet 9: Site Plan (overhead utilities shown on south only, north omitted)\n- Sheet 31: Landscape Plan (overhead utilities shown, trees planted 10-15 feet away)\n- Sheet 21: Preliminary Electrical Design Plan (underground only, not relevant)\n- Sheet 30: Existing Tree Plan\n- Sheets 1-39: Complete site plan set\n\n**GROUPING REQUIREMENTS:**\nThe grouping (EL-MD-EXP) requires:\n- Site Plan Sheets with existing/proposed facilities\n- Building Elevation Sheets with surveyed conductor elevations and clearance dimensions\n- Section/Profile Exhibits with clearance dimensions\n- Utility Plans with clearance annotations\n- Landscape Plans with tree species verification\n- Cover Sheet notes including 48-hour notification requirement\n\n**YOUR TASK:**\nEvaluate each of the 47 checklist items (EL-1.1 through EL-1.47) based on this evidence. For each item, determine:\n- **Status**: pass (compliant), fail (non-compliant), not-verifiable (cannot determine), or n/a (not applicable)\n- **Applicable Areas**: Where on the site the item applies (e.g., \"southern property boundary\", \"proposed buildings 1-3\", etc.)\n- **Code Citation**: The UCM or ECM section from the checklist\n- **Comment**: What was found, what's missing, or why it cannot be verified\n\nFocus on items where:\n1. Evidence shows a clear violation (fail)\n2. Critical evidence is missing preventing verification (not-verifiable)\n3. The item genuinely doesn't apply to this project (n/a)\n\nOutput your evaluation in a structured format for each item. Be thorough - this is the complete set of checklist items for this grouping."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":9,"cache_creation_input_tokens":768,"cache_read_input_tokens":64487,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":768,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ef672522-d4e7-4b95-adea-0f34cf5c6c5b","uuid":"f0ee0a22-1b93-4f03-b950-6dedb4bb99dc"}
{"level":30,"time":1777032041554,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01LhtdwAFCHd55XuhNETGdkc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The landscape plan doesn't specify which tree species the M-labeled (mitigation) trees are. I need to work with what I know:\n\nFrom the plant schedule (Sheet 35):\n- 10 Cedar Elm (100 gal., 4\" cal. = 4 inches caliper)\n- 2 Live Oak (100 gal., 4\" cal. = 4 inches caliper)\n- 6 Mexican Redbud (45 gal., 2.5\" cal.)\n- 2 Texas Mountain Laurel (45 gal., 2.5\" cal.)\n\nThe Cedar Elm and Live Oak are specified at 4\" caliper, which far exceeds the 1.5\" threshold for \"excess caliper inches counting towards mitigation\". So the 12 shade trees would be the main mitigation trees.\n\nThe non-utility-compatible, large-tree issues are:\n1. Cedar Elm - 90 ft mature height (large, non-UC)\n2. Live Oak - 60 ft mature height (large, non-UC)\n\nThese 12 shade trees are within 3-6 feet of the OHE line.\n\nBut I can't definitively say which specific M-labeled trees are Cedar Elm vs Live Oak without species labeling on the landscape plan. This affects EL-2.10 (plant legend identifies species) and EL-2.11 (large trees differentiated).\n\nLet me now compile my findings for the output. Key failures/not-verifiable items:\n\n**FAIL items:**\n1. EL-2.1 - Non-utility-compatible trees (Cedar Elm, Live Oak per plant schedule) within 3-6 feet of OHE distribution conductor (must be 15+ feet)\n2. EL-2.3 - Large trees (Cedar Elm 90 ft, Live Oak 60 ft) within 3-6 feet of OHE distribution conductor (must be 25+ feet)  \n\n\n3. EL-2.15 - Trees proposed within restricted clearance zones without documented Austin Energy written approval\n\n**NOT-VERIFIABLE items:**\n1. EL-2.6 - Utility poles not clearly marked on the plans\n2. EL-2.7 - Can't confirm exact tree positioning relative to transformer pads, though visually appears acceptable\n3. EL-2.8 - Underground equipment locations and required tree setbacks aren't specifically documented\n4. EL-2.9 - Same constraint as EL-2.8\n5. EL-2.10 - Plant schedule lists species on Sheet 35, but the landscape plan itself lacks species identification on individual tree symbols\n6. EL-2.11 - Mature heights and specific species aren't noted on the landscape plan; only regulatory categories appear\n7. EL-2.12 - Clearance dimensions aren't shown on the plans themselves\n8. EL-2.13 - Same issue—clearances lack dimensional notation\n\nFor EL-2.10, the plant schedule on Sheet 35 does list species, but Sheet 31 only shows regulatory categories without cross-referencing those species directly. The landscape plan itself doesn't identify which species correspond to each tree location, even though that information exists elsewhere. EL-2.11 has a similar problem—Sheet 35 provides tree specifications like size and caliper, but the landscape plan doesn't differentiate large trees or show their mature heights, making it difficult to verify clearances from overhead electric lines without referencing another sheet. does identify species on the plant schedule, but the landscape plan itself lacks a species legend—it only shows regulatory categories (SY, PL, P, M) without identifying what trees those symbols represent. The applicability for EL-2.10 specifically asks about the landscape plan legend's completeness, which means the species identification needs to be visible there, not just on a separate schedule. So EL-2.10 appears to be a failure since the plan legend doesn't provide species names. For EL-2.11, the issue is that large trees (those exceeding 40 feet at maturity per ECM standards) aren't differentiated on the plans, making it impossible to verify adequate clearances from overhead electric facilities. The plan only shows generic symbols without indicating which trees are large or their mature heights. This also fails the requirement. My assessment identifies five failures: the non-utility-compatible species (Cedar Elm and Live Oak) positioned too close to overhead conductors, the landscape plan legend lacking species identification, large trees not differentiated on the plan, and trees within restricted clearance zones without documented Austin Energy approval. Two items cannot be verified because utility poles aren't clearly marked and transformer locations can't be definitively cross-referenced to tree positions. utes \"failure\" for these items.\n\nEL-2.10 requires the plant legend to identify tree species near electric facilities. The landscape plan legend only shows category abbreviations (SY, PL, P, M) without species names. While a plant schedule exists on Sheet 35, the landscape plan itself contains no reference directing users to it, so the legend doesn't independently satisfy the requirement.\n\nTherefore, EL-2.10 should be marked as a failure.\n\nEL-2.11 demands that large trees (40+ feet mature height per ECM Appendix F) be differentiated on the plans themselves to verify clearances from overhead electric lines. The landscape plan uses an M symbol for mitigation trees but doesn't distinguish which ones exceed the 40-foot threshold—that information only appears on the separate plant schedule. Since the requirement specifies differentiation \"on plans,\" this also constitutes a failure.\n\nBoth items warrant FAIL designations. I should verify whether I'm applying the standard correctly by reviewing the grouping criteria about missing or unverifiable elements.\n\nThe landscape plan must enable verification of tree clearances without requiring cross-reference to other sheets. Species identification and size classification need to be present on Sheet 31 itself for compliance. Without this, the plan cannot demonstrate adherence to the clearance requirements.\n\nThe findings break down as six failures: non-utility-compatible species within 15 feet of distribution conductors at measured distances of 3-6 feet (EL-2.1), large trees similarly positioned (EL-2.3), missing species identification in the plant legend (EL-2.10), and large trees not differentiated on the landscape plan (EL-2.11). the landscape plan itself, even if a plant schedule exists elsewhere. The requirement seems to expect species identification to be accessible from the landscape plan document. So EL-2.10 is a deficiency.\n\nFor EL-2.11, the same logic applies—the landscape plan should show mature heights, but it doesn't. The plant schedule on Sheet 35 may contain this information, but the landscape plan itself lacks it. This is also a deficiency.\n\nHowever, I should reconsider what \"not-verifiable\" means in this context. The instructions indicate marking something as not-verifiable when evidence is absent or ambiguous. Here, the evidence isn't absent—Sheet 35 provides the plant schedule with species and heights. The question becomes whether providing this information on a separate sheet satisfies the requirement. If the requirement can be verified through available documents (even if cross-referenced), then it's verifiable, not absent. The issue is whether the applicant has adequately met the requirement's intent, which may be a FAIL rather than NOT-VERIFIABLE.\n\nThe core problem is that while species are documented on Sheet 35, there's no clear link showing which of those species correspond to the trees positioned near the electric facilities on the landscape plan. The M symbols marking tree locations near utilities aren't connected to specific species designations. This creates ambiguity about compliance—I can see trees are proposed near utilities and I can see a species list exists, but I cannot confirm which species are actually placed in those utility-adjacent locations. This ambiguity about what's actually being proposed near the facilities makes EL-2.10 NOT-VERIFIABLE.\n\nThe same issue applies to EL-2.11 regarding tree heights. The plant schedule indicates Cedar Elm and Live Oak reach 90 and 60 feet respectively, and these appear to be the primary mitigation trees based on quantities and their designation as shade trees. However, without explicit documentation linking these species to the M symbols on the plan, I'm making logical inferences rather than verifying documented facts. The grouping description mentions incomplete conductor locations and site plan utility pole information, which suggests there may be other documentation gaps affecting the overall utility clearance verification.\n\nFor EL-2.10, the species are technically identified in the plant schedule on Sheet 35, so the requirement has been addressed. But the landscape plan itself doesn't specify species near utilities, which creates a gap between what's documented and what's shown where it matters most. For EL-2.11, without explicit notation of which trees meet the 40+ foot threshold on the landscape plan, I can't verify compliance with the large tree clearance requirements.\n\nBoth items fall into the \"could not verify\" category from my earlier analysis—trees near the overhead line lack species identification, large trees aren't distinguished from smaller ones, and no clearance dimensions are provided. This means EL-2.10 and EL-2.11 should both be marked NOT-VERIFIABLE.\n\nFor the final determination, EL-2.1 fails because non-utility-compatible trees are positioned within 15 feet of the distribution conductor. EL-2.3 also fails since large trees sit within 25 feet of the distribution conductor. Now I need to reconsider EL-2.15—it only applies when trees are actually proposed in restricted zones. Since EL-2.1 and EL-2.3 already confirm trees are in those restricted zones, EL-2.15 becomes applicable. The requirement asks for explicit written approval from Austin Energy authorizing any variation, and no such approval letter appears in the documentation, so this item also fails.\n\nThe remaining failures are EL-2.10 (species cannot be verified, only regulatory categories are shown) and EL-2.11 (large trees aren't differentiated on the plan to indicate which reach 40+ feet at maturity).\n\nFor items that cannot be verified: poles aren't depicted on the plans to confirm 10-foot clearances, transformer pad locations lack cross-sheet dimensions to assess tree proximity, underground equipment isn't detailed enough to verify either the 5-foot clearances or root barrier requirements, and clearances generally aren't dimensioned on the drawings.\n\nThe remaining items either don't apply—no transmission facilities or easements exist—or pass, since trees and utilities are both shown on the landscape plan. Let me reconsider EL-2.10 against the actual requirement. The deficiency states the plant legend or schedule must identify species near electric facilities. The plant schedule on Sheet 35 does list specific species like Cedar Elm and Live Oak, while the legend on Sheet 31 only shows regulatory categories. Since the schedule identifies species, this might actually satisfy the requirement rather than fail it. present on the landscape plan. → FAIL\n\nBut the checklist offers \"Plant legend OR plant schedule\" as alternatives—either one can satisfy the requirement. Since Sheet 35's plant schedule identifies species, the requirement is met regardless of what's on the landscape plan legend. The deficiency isn't present → EL-2.10 should be PASS.\n\nFor EL-2.11, the requirement is that large trees must be differentiated on the plans. Sheet 35 identifies which species are large (Cedar Elm at 90 ft, Live Oak at 60 ft), but Sheet 31 doesn't indicate size differentiation. So large trees aren't distinguished on the actual landscape plan, meaning the deficiency is present → EL-2.11 should be FAIL.\n\nWait—the checklist says to check \"Landscape plan and plant schedule for large tree designation or mature height notation.\" It's asking me to look at both documents. The plant schedule clearly marks the large trees with their mature heights. That satisfies the requirement, so the deficiency isn't present → PASS.\n\nI need to step back and consider what these requirements actually accomplish: they exist so anyone reviewing the plans can quickly identify which trees near utilities are appropriate species and confirm adequate clearances. The plant schedule serves this purpose by documenting species and mature heights.\n\nFor EL-2.10, the landscape plan shows tree locations near overhead electric facilities but doesn't label species, while the plant schedule lists all species separately. There's no clear way to cross-reference which specific trees are which species without making assumptions. For EL-2.11, the same issue applies—the plan doesn't indicate which trees are large, and while the schedule provides sizes, there's no explicit connection between the two documents. Both items require verifiable information that links the landscape plan to the plant schedule, which isn't clearly provided.\n\nThis aligns with the broader pattern: when critical compliance details exist somewhere in the submission but aren't visibly connected to the site plan, they're effectively unverifiable. The final assessment identifies three clear failures where non-compliant trees are documented within restricted distances, and eight items that cannot be verified due to missing cross-references or incomplete documentation on the plans themselves.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":11,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1068,"cache_read_input_tokens":67707,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1068,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"13d31a74-8d60-4982-8b24-9aff9d56548a","uuid":"4a5332df-dddd-4241-bffa-6a5e631e1ed3"}
{"level":30,"time":1777032041578,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a9756397ccdf4fe82","tool_use_id":"toolu_013TYaEkp4jKyikLtXBbFZe5","description":"Evaluate all 47 overhead electric clearance checklist items","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"I'm conducting a site plan review for overhead electric clearance requirements (EL-MD-EXP grouping, 47 checklist items total). I have completed research on the Valley View Townhomes project and found the following key facts:\n\n**EXISTING FACILITIES:**\n- Overhead utility line documented on-site along northern property boundary with a utility pole (Sheet 4)\n- Overhead electric line on southern property boundary (Sheets 9, 31)\n- Proposed 8 buildings with various elevations (686.25-685 ft MSL range)\n- Proposed driveways crossing under overhead lines on southern boundary\n- Proposed trees (mitigation trees) near southern overhead line, approximately 10-15 feet away\n- NO building elevations provided to verify vertical clearances\n- NO clearance dimension callouts on any sheets\n- NO surveyed conductor elevations shown\n- NO 48-hour notification note found on cover sheet\n- Discrepancy: Northern overhead utilities on existing survey (Sheet 4) are omitted from proposed site plan (Sheet 9) with no demolition/relocation notes\n\n**DOCUMENTS AVAILABLE:**\n- Sheet 4: Existing Conditions Survey (overhead utilities shown)\n- Sheet 9: Site Plan (overhead utilities shown on south only, north omitted)\n- Sheet 31: Landscape Plan (overhead utilities shown, trees planted 10-15 feet away)\n- Sheet 21: Preliminary Electrical Design Plan (underground only, not relevant)\n- Sheet 30: Existing Tree Plan\n- Sheets 1-39: Complete site plan set\n\n**GROUPING REQUIREMENTS:**\nThe grouping (EL-MD-EXP) requires:\n- Site Plan Sheets with existing/proposed facilities\n- Building Elevation Sheets with surveyed conductor elevations and clearance dimensions\n- Section/Profile Exhibits with clearance dimensions\n- Utility Plans with clearance annotations\n- Landscape Plans with tree species verification\n- Cover Sheet notes including 48-hour notification requirement\n\n**YOUR TASK:**\nEvaluate each of the 47 checklist items (EL-1.1 through EL-1.47) based on this evidence. For each item, determine:\n- **Status**: pass (compliant), fail (non-compliant), not-verifiable (cannot determine), or n/a (not applicable)\n- **Applicable Areas**: Where on the site the item applies (e.g., \"southern property boundary\", \"proposed buildings 1-3\", etc.)\n- **Code Citation**: The UCM or ECM section from the checklist\n- **Comment**: What was found, what's missing, or why it cannot be verified\n\nFocus on items where:\n1. Evidence shows a clear violation (fail)\n2. Critical evidence is missing preventing verification (not-verifiable)\n3. The item genuinely doesn't apply to this project (n/a)\n\nOutput your evaluation in a structured format for each item. Be thorough - this is the complete set of checklist items for this grouping.","uuid":"fb4ec69b-5974-4581-8834-f60031ecb574","session_id":"ef672522-d4e7-4b95-adea-0f34cf5c6c5b"}
{"level":30,"time":1777032041578,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'm conducting a site plan review for overhead electric clearance requirements (EL-MD-EXP grouping, 47 checklist items total). I have completed research on the Valley View Townhomes project and found the following key facts:\n\n**EXISTING FACILITIES:**\n- Overhead utility line documented on-site along northern property boundary with a utility pole (Sheet 4)\n- Overhead electric line on southern property boundary (Sheets 9, 31)\n- Proposed 8 buildings with various elevations (686.25-685 ft MSL range)\n- Proposed driveways crossing under overhead lines on southern boundary\n- Proposed trees (mitigation trees) near southern overhead line, approximately 10-15 feet away\n- NO building elevations provided to verify vertical clearances\n- NO clearance dimension callouts on any sheets\n- NO surveyed conductor elevations shown\n- NO 48-hour notification note found on cover sheet\n- Discrepancy: Northern overhead utilities on existing survey (Sheet 4) are omitted from proposed site plan (Sheet 9) with no demolition/relocation notes\n\n**DOCUMENTS AVAILABLE:**\n- Sheet 4: Existing Conditions Survey (overhead utilities shown)\n- Sheet 9: Site Plan (overhead utilities shown on south only, north omitted)\n- Sheet 31: Landscape Plan (overhead utilities shown, trees planted 10-15 feet away)\n- Sheet 21: Preliminary Electrical Design Plan (underground only, not relevant)\n- Sheet 30: Existing Tree Plan\n- Sheets 1-39: Complete site plan set\n\n**GROUPING REQUIREMENTS:**\nThe grouping (EL-MD-EXP) requires:\n- Site Plan Sheets with existing/proposed facilities\n- Building Elevation Sheets with surveyed conductor elevations and clearance dimensions\n- Section/Profile Exhibits with clearance dimensions\n- Utility Plans with clearance annotations\n- Landscape Plans with tree species verification\n- Cover Sheet notes including 48-hour notification requirement\n\n**YOUR TASK:**\nEvaluate each of the 47 checklist items (EL-1.1 through EL-1.47) based on this evidence. For each item, determine:\n- **Status**: pass (compliant), fail (non-compliant), not-verifiable (cannot determine), or n/a (not applicable)\n- **Applicable Areas**: Where on the site the item applies (e.g., \"southern property boundary\", \"proposed buildings 1-3\", etc.)\n- **Code Citation**: The UCM or ECM section from the checklist\n- **Comment**: What was found, what's missing, or why it cannot be verified\n\nFocus on items where:\n1. Evidence shows a clear violation (fail)\n2. Critical evidence is missing preventing verification (not-verifiable)\n3. The item genuinely doesn't apply to this project (n/a)\n\nOutput your evaluation in a structured format for each item. Be thorough - this is the complete set of checklist items for this grouping."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013TYaEkp4jKyikLtXBbFZe5","session_id":"ef672522-d4e7-4b95-adea-0f34cf5c6c5b","uuid":"9591d6f8-4a2a-413d-9da3-02272f8c32ff"}
{"level":30,"time":1777032042798,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":21,"prompt":"Please examine the electrical plan and identify:\n\n1. Are there any overhead electric utility lines shown on this plan? If yes, describe their location and routing relative to the transformer pads.\n2. What is the source of power shown for each transformer pad (e.g., underground lines only, or overhead lines)?\n3. Are there any dashed lines indicating future/proposed utility routes that might cross transformer locations?\n4. Are there any notes about utility easements (PUE, electrical easements) relative to transformer pads?\n5. Is there a clear electrical routing path shown from each transformer to a meter location?\n6. Are all electrical lines from transformers to meters clearly shown as being on private property?\n7. Are there any markings for niche service, enclosed areas, or notched-out building configurations where any transformer might be located?\n8. Is there any indication that these are oil-filled or dry-type transformers? Look for any \"OIL\" or \"DRY\" or liquid containment markings.\n9. Are there any cross-section references or elevation callouts that would show vertical clearances?\n10. Are there specific building windows, doors, or ventilating ducts labeled near any transformer location that would require clearance verification?","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visual analysis of the provided Preliminary Electrical Design Plan, here are the answers to your questions:\n\n1.  **Are there any overhead electric utility lines shown on this plan? If yes, describe their location and routing relative to the transformer pads.**\n    Yes, there are overhead electric lines indicated off-site. On the far western (left) edge of the drawing, outside the main property boundary, there is a line labeled **\"- OHE -\"** (Overhead Electric) running roughly north-south. The on-site transformer pads (located near the northwest entrance and along the western and eastern edges of the property) do not have overhead lines routed directly to or over them on the plan; they appear to be fed by underground lines that originate from the direction of this off-site OHE line.\n\n2.  **What is the source of power shown for each transformer pad (e.g., underground lines only, or overhead lines)?**\n    The power routed on-site to each transformer pad is shown exclusively via underground lines. These are depicted as solid blue lines with an 'E' symbol, and in some areas, dashed blue lines specifically called out with a leader line as **\"Underground Electric Line\"** (e.g., near the northwest transformer and between the southern pull boxes).\n\n3.  **Are there any dashed lines indicating future/proposed utility routes that might cross transformer locations?**\n    No. There are dashed lines representing other utilities (like wastewater or storm drainage) and dashed blue lines for underground electric, but none of these are shown intersecting or crossing over the rectangular footprints labeled **\"Electrical Transformer Pad\"**.\n\n4.  **Are there any notes about utility easements (PUE, electrical easements) relative to transformer pads?**\n    Yes. Specifically, adjacent to the **\"Electrical Transformer Pad\"** located on the west side of the site near Building 8, there is a dashed rectangular boundary with a callout label reading **\"Vault Easement\"**. There is also a **\"15' Elec. & Tele. Easement\"** noted along the southern property boundary, though no transformer pads are located within it.\n\n5.  **Is there a clear electrical routing path shown from each transformer to a meter location?**\n    Yes. Solid blue lines with an 'E' symbol clearly trace the routing paths from each **\"Electrical Transformer Pad\"** to various **\"Electrical Pull Box\"** locations, which then branch out to connect to the rectangular boxes labeled **\"Electrical Meters\"** attached to the buildings.\n\n6.  **Are all electrical lines from transformers to meters clearly shown as being on private property?**\n    Yes. All the solid blue electrical routing lines connecting the on-site transformers to the building meters are contained within the heavy dot-dash line that demarcates the site's property boundary.\n\n7.  **Are there any markings for niche service, enclosed areas, or notched-out building configurations where any transformer might be located?**\n    No. The three **\"Electrical Transformer Pad\"** locations shown (northwest corner, west side near Bldg 8, and east side between Bldgs 4 & 5) all appear to be sited in open exterior areas, such as landscape buffers or adjacent to driveways/parking, rather than within building niches or enclosures.\n\n8.  **Is there any indication that these are oil-filled or dry-type transformers? Look for any \"OIL\" or \"DRY\" or liquid containment markings.**\n    No. The pads are uniformly labeled simply as **\"Electrical Transformer Pad\"**. There are no labels indicating \"OIL\", \"DRY\", or any markings depicting liquid containment areas around the pads.\n\n9.  **Are there any cross-section references or elevation callouts that would show vertical clearances?**\n    No. The plan provides Finished Floor Elevations (e.g., **\"FFE = 687.50\"**) for the buildings and shows topographical contour lines, but there are no cross-section cut markers or specific elevation callouts detailing vertical clearances for the electrical equipment.\n\n10. **Are there specific building windows, doors, or ventilating ducts labeled near any transformer location that would require clearance verification?**\n    No. The building footprints show the overall outline and unit demising lines (e.g., **\"Unit 801\"**, **\"Unit 802\"**), but architectural features such as windows, doors, or ventilating ducts are not drawn or labeled on this electrical site plan.","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1564,"outputTokens":943,"totalTokens":4985,"reasoningTokens":2478},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 2092056]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisually analyze the binary file provided with extreme accuracy. Pay close attention to tables, reproducing them EXACTLY as they appear, character-by-character.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not paraphrase, interpret, or \"fill in\" text. If you cannot read something clearly, state \"text unclear\" rather than guessing. Transcribe field labels and values exactly as written.\n\nIf the requirements being validated require certain content to be present, look closely for it and provide all relevant information that pertains to the broader request. Remember that you are only looking at a single supplementary document, or a single page from the primary site plan. Just because you do not find evidence for a requirement on this page does not mean it will not be present on another page.\n\nReport back precisely on what you see, using exact quotes for any text you transcribe from forms, tables, or labels.\n\nThe final review agent will not be able to look at this document directly, so you must be their eyes. Ensure you describe everything relevant in full detail with perfect accuracy.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Please examine the electrical plan and identify:\n\n1. Are there any overhead electric utility lines shown on this plan? If yes, describe their location and routing relative to the transformer pads.\n2. What is the source of power shown for each transformer pad (e.g., underground lines only, or overhead lines)?\n3. Are there any dashed lines indicating future/proposed utility routes that might cross transformer locations?\n4. Are there any notes about utility easements (PUE, electrical easements) relative to transformer pads?\n5. Is there a clear electrical routing path shown from each transformer to a meter location?\n6. Are all electrical lines from transformers to meters clearly shown as being on private property?\n7. Are there any markings for niche service, enclosed areas, or notched-out building configurations where any transformer might be located?\n8. Is there any indication that these are oil-filled or dry-type transformers? Look for any \"OIL\" or \"DRY\" or liquid containment markings.\n9. Are there any cross-section references or elevation callouts that would show vertical clearances?\n10. Are there specific building windows, doors, or ventilating ducts labeled near any transformer location that would require clearance verification?"}]}],"headers":{"user-agent":"ai/5.0.152"}}},"response":{"id":"aitxt-P5IFiEAjBPhrmfQG2sgF6PTk","timestamp":{},"modelId":"google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview","headers":{"access-control-allow-headers":"Content-Type, Authorization, ai-gateway-auth-method, ai-gateway-protocol-version, ai-model-id, ai-language-model-id, ai-language-model-specification-version, ai-image-model-specification-version, ai-embedding-model-specification-version, ai-language-model-streaming, ai-reporting-tags, ai-reporting-user, http-referer, x-title, anthropic-beta","access-control-allow-methods":"GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, OPTIONS","access-control-allow-origin":"*","cache-control":"public, max-age=0, must-revalidate","content-encoding":"br","content-type":"application/json","date":"Fri, 24 Apr 2026 12:00:42 GMT","server":"Vercel","strict-transport-security":"max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload","transfer-encoding":"chunked","vary":"rsc, next-router-state-tree, next-router-prefetch, next-router-segment-prefetch","x-matched-path":"/v1/ai/language-model","x-vercel-cache":"MISS","x-vercel-id":"iad1::iad1::zjnnl-1777031987366-a5fd2cae1001"},"body":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a visu