{"level":30,"time":1777032048871,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Eaq6rjDcb8S3coSKC6rrP2","type":"tool_result","content":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/glossary/display-spaces.md:- **Related to EL-1.19:** The electrical review guide (EL-1.19) also references \"vehicle parking or display areas on concrete pads\" in the context of overhead electric line clearances (UCM § 1.10.6.2), confirming that display areas are a distinct physical space type recognized in city review practice.\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/glossary/electrical-easement.md:- **Applicability trigger for el/1.md items 14–15 (EL-1.27)**: The check triggers when electric easement boundaries are shown on the plat/easement exhibit AND the easement contains or is adjacent to overhead distribution facilities. Items within the easement must maintain 7.5-foot clearance from the outside conductor; no customer facilities may be installed under or over overhead distribution lines regardless of easement status.\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/glossary/special-height-restriction-zones.md:- **Measurement from conductor, not pole:** The single most common error in delineating these zones is measuring from the utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost conductor. On three-phase installations, the outside conductor may be several feet offset from the pole center. Plans showing clearances measured from pole centerline are non-compliant even if the distances appear sufficient. This is separately flagged as EL-1.23 in the el/1.md review guide.\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/glossary/staging-areas.md:- **Two distinct meanings of \"staging area\" appear in Austin codes.** The DCM staging area is a *permanent* designated maintenance area adjacent to an SCM (detention/retention pond). A separate concept — *construction staging area* — appears in ECM § 2.7.2 (temporary disturbance during construction, including job shacks and worker parking) and LDC § 25-2-754(I) (temporary off-site construction staging within 500 ft of the construction site). These construction staging areas are subject to OSHA 10-ft working clearance from energized overhead lines (UCM § 1.10.2 / EL-1.45), not the DCM 14-ft clearance. **Do not conflate these two concepts.** The 14-ft clearance requirement applies exclusively to the permanent SCM maintenance staging area under DCM § 1.2.4.E.2.\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/glossary/truck-traffic-areas.md:- **Review items EL-1.18 and EL-1.19** in el/1.md both apply this determination: driveways (EL-1.18) and parking pads (EL-1.19) each require the reviewer to assess whether truck traffic applies to set the correct clearance threshold.\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.1 | Buildings and permanent structures do not maintain minimum 7 feet 6 inches horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from overhead electric conductors | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings or permanent structures are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for overhead poles/conductors and building locations; verify clearance measured from outside conductor, not pole. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities do not maintain minimum 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductor of overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when customer facilities/installations of any type (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other installations) are proposed and overhead distribution facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and utility plan for overhead distribution lines (voltage <60kV, typically 12.47kV or 34.5kV in Austin) and facility setbacks; verify conductor type labeled as primary or neutral. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.3 | Building projections (overhangs, awnings, balconies, decks, roofs, patios) do not maintain required clearances from overhead electric conductors | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when building projections are shown and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Building elevations and section views for projections extending toward overhead conductors; verify both 7.5-foot sky-to-ground and 15-foot radial clearances from outside conductor. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.4 | Gas pump canopies do not maintain required clearances from overhead electric lines | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when gas pump canopies are proposed and overhead electric lines exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for gas pump canopy locations and overhead line routing; verify both 7.5-foot sky-to-ground and 15-foot radial clearances. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.5 | Stairs, elevated walkways, and elevated platforms do not maintain minimum 15-foot radial clearance from overhead electric distribution facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when stairs, elevated walkways, or elevated platforms are proposed and overhead electric distribution facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and section views for elevated features near overhead lines; verify 15-foot radial clearance from outside conductor. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.6 | Buildings do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance and 15-foot radial clearance from overhead electric facilities in right-of-way adjacent to site | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings are proposed and electric facilities exist or will be relocated in ROW adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for building setback from ROW and utility plan for electric facility locations or relocation notes; verify clearances are permanent requirements per UCM § 1.10.3, distinct from construction working clearances. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.7 | Surveyed elevations and locations of overhead conductors not included on building elevation sheets to demonstrate required clearances are met | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings or elevated structures are proposed within potential clearance distance of overhead electric facilities. Check: Building elevation sheets for surveyed conductor elevation data and clearance dimension annotations; verify survey performed by registered surveyor. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.8 | Section and plan view exhibits do not show dimensions from outer electric conductor to outermost edge of buildings and building projections for clearance verification | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when buildings, permanent structures, or building projections are proposed within potential clearance distance of overhead electric facilities. Check: Section views and enlarged plan details for dimension lines showing clearances from outer conductor to building edges. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.9 | Fences do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from outside conductor of overhead electric facilities | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when fences are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for fence alignments and dimension annotations showing clearance from outside conductor (not pole). |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.10 | Fence posts do not maintain minimum 10-foot horizontal clearance from overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when fences are proposed and overhead electric poles exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan for fence post locations relative to electric pole locations. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.11 | Fences connecting to or near substation fences not electrically isolated with 10-foot non-conductive isolation panel connecting the two fences | UCM § 1.14.0 | Only when fences are proposed adjacent to or connecting with existing Austin Energy substation fences. Check: Site plan for fence alignments near substation property and detail sheets for 10-foot non-conductive (wooden) panel specifications between metal fence and substation fence. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.12 | Fencing near electric transmission lines not constructed of non-conductive material or not properly grounded, or specifications and grounding details not provided | UCM § 1.14.0 | Only when fencing is proposed beneath or adjacent to electric transmission lines (voltage ≥60kV). Check: Site plan for transmission line proximity to fences (facilities labeled 69kV, 138kV, or 345kV, or within transmission easement) and detail sheets for fence material specifications and grounding detail drawings. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.13 | Vehicle barrier fencing beneath overhead electric lines does not maintain minimum 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance from outermost electric line or lacks vertical and horizontal clearance verification exhibits | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when vehicle barrier posts or fencing are proposed beneath or adjacent to overhead electric lines. Check: Site plan for barrier locations beneath overhead lines and section exhibits showing clearance dimensions from outermost conductor. |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.14 | Retaining walls do not maintain minimum 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from outermost electric line where overhead facilities are present | UCM § 1.10.3 | Only when retaining walls are proposed and overhead electric facilities exist on or adjacent to site. Check: Site plan and grading plan for retaining wall alignments and clearance dimensions from outermost electric line (measured from conductor, not pole). |\n/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el-md-exp/1.md:| EL-1.15 | Retaining walls adjacent to Austin Energy substation walls do not maintain minimum 5-foot separation to allow for grounding grid extension, wall inspection, wall replacement, and proper drainage | Reviewer Convention | Only when retaining walls are proposed adjacent to Austin Energy substation property. Check: Site plan for retaining wall locations within 10 feet of substation boundary and clearance dimension from substation wall. |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013TYaEkp4jKyikLtXBbFZe5","session_id":"ef672522-d4e7-4b95-adea-0f34cf5c6c5b","uuid":"0a92ad74-3e7e-4223-bba2-c023f6361d09"}
{"level":30,"time":1777032048878,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NSVDhzREsgCdaHQJzifMAq","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 19 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: diagram — A legend explaining the symbols and line types used in the site plan diagram for various utilities.\n     4→\n     5→### Legend\n     6→| Symbol | Description |\n     7→| :--- | :--- |\n     8→| W | Existing Water Line |\n     9→| W | Proposed Water Line |\n    10→| [Solid Square Symbol] | Single Water Service |\n    11→| [Split Square Symbol] | Double Water Service |\n    12→| WW | Existing Wastewater Line |\n    13→| WW | Proposed Wastewater Line |\n    14→| [Dashed Line] | Wastewater Service |\n    15→| RW | Existing Reclaimed Water Line |\n    16→| RW | Proposed Reclaimed Water Line |\n    17→| E | Proposed Underground Electric |\n    18→| SD | Proposed Storm Drain |\n    19→| [FH Symbol] | Fire Hydrant |\n    20→| [Valve Symbol] | Gate Valve |\n    21→| [AARV Symbol] | AARV |\n    22→| [T Symbol] | Transformer Pad |\n    23→\n    24→## Block 2: drawing — The main diagram showing the layout of the townhomes, including buildings, units, utility lines (water, wastewater, electric, storm drain), fire hydrants, cleanouts, and other site features. It includes numerous annotations and specific measurements for the utility infrastructure.\n    25→\n    26→[Detailed diagram showing the layout of multiple buildings labeled Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8, each containing multiple units. The diagram includes extensive utility routing with lines for water (W), wastewater (WW), and storm drain (SD). There are numerous callouts for specific components like '6\" Cleanout Traffic Bearing', 'Air Spade Trench', 'Duplex Grinder Pump Lift Station', 'Fire Demand Meter', and 'PVC Wastewater Service Line'. Topographic lines and elevations are also visible. Various easements and setbacks are marked. The layout is bounded by 'Valley View Road' to the west.]\n    27→\n    28→## Block 3: notes — The legal description of the property.\n    29→\n    30→### Legal Description\n    31→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    32→\n    33→## Block 4: notes — A note detailing the requirements for restraining joints on water line components.\n    34→\n    35→### Restrained Joints for all Water Line Bends, Tees, Gate Valves & Ends\n    36→All horizontal and vertical water line bends, tee's, gate valves and dead end's shall be restrained to the water main using factory restrained joint pipe as approved in SPL WW-27F, or, mechanical joint restraint devices as approved in SPL WW-27A.\n    37→\n    38→## Block 5: notes — A disclaimer regarding the accuracy of existing utility locations shown on the plans, requiring the contractor to field verify.\n    39→\n    40→The location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n    41→\n    42→## Block 6: notes — A brief note directing the reader to the landscape plan sheet for the tree list.\n    43→\n    44→### Tree List\n    45→Refer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n    46→\n    47→## Block 7: notes — A table listing the Drainage Fixture Units (DFU's) for each building, broken down by building type (e.g., 4Plex, 5Plex, 6Plex).\n    48→\n    49→### Drainage Fixture Unit Table\n    50→| Building # | Building Type | DFU's |\n    51→| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n    52→| 1 | 4Plex | 136 |\n    53→| 2 | 6Plex | 204 |\n    54→| 3 | 5Plex | 170 |\n    55→| 4 | 5Plex | 170 |\n    56→| 5 | 4Plex | 136 |\n    57→| 6 | 4Plex | 136 |\n    58→| 7 | 5Plex | 170 |\n    59→| 8 | 6Plex | 204 |\n    60→\n    61→## Block 8: notes — A note indicating the requirement for a pressure reducing valve for lots/structures with high water pressure.\n    62→\n    63→### PRV Note\n    64→Lots/Structure with 65 psi or greater require a pressure reducing valve, set at 65 psi, to be installed on the property owner's side of the right of way.\n    65→\n    66→## Block 9: notes\n    67→\n    68→## Block 11: table — The logo and contact information for Bleyl Engineering, the firm responsible for the planning, design, and management of the project.\n    69→\n    70→### Bleyl Engineering\n    71→Planning • Design • Management\n    72→7701 San Felipe Blvd., Suite 200, Austin TX 78729\n    73→Texas Firm Registration No. F-678\n    74→Tel. 512-454-2400\n    75→www.bleylengineering.com\n    76→\n    77→Austin | Bryan | Conroe | Houston\n    78→\n    79→## Block 12: table — A note regarding the requirements for private fire hydrants, including compliance with the Austin Fire Code and annual maintenance.\n    80→\n    81→### City of Austin - Water and Wastewater Utility - Special Services Division\n    82→(512) 972-1060\n    83→This project has private hydrants located within the property. The property owner is required to comply with Austin Fire Code. Failure to comply may result in civil and/or criminal remedies available to the City. The performance of this obligation shall always rest with the owner or record. Fire hydrants on private property are required to serviced, maintained and flowed annually, using a contractor registered with the City to provide this service. This project includes __1__ private hydrants.\n    84→\n    85→## Block 13: text_block\n    86→\n    87→## Block 14: text_block — A disclaimer regarding the verification of data and calculations, stating the engineer's responsibility.\n    88→\n    89→Release of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n    90→\n    91→## Block 16: text_block — Information about the benchmarks used for the project, including elevation and location details.\n    92→\n    93→### Benchmarks\n    94→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    95→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    96→\n    97→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    98→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    99→\n   100→## Block 19: text_block — A list of utility notes instructing the contractor to verify existing utility locations, notify the engineer of conflicts, and specifying pipe materials for private domestic water and wastewater branches.\n   101→\n   102→### Utility Notes\n   103→1. Contractor shall verify horizontal and vertical location of existing utilities prior to construction.\n   104→2. Contractor shall verify location of existing line, point of connection and notify engineer of any conflicts/discrepancies prior to installation of improvements.\n   105→3. All Private Domestic Water Branches to the buildings are to be 2\" HDPE service tubing. See Detail 520-AW-01B\n   106→4. All Private Wastewater Branches to the buildings are to be 6\" PVC @ 2.0% min. See Detail 520-AW-01C\n   107→\n   108→## Block 20: text_block — A general note stating that the project is subject to the Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule and requires inspection by a geologist for trenches deeper than 5 feet.\n   109→\n   110→### Note\n   111→This project is subject to the Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule (COA ECM 1.12.0 and COA Item No. 658S of the SSM) provision that all trenching greater than 5 feet deep must be inspected by a geologist (Texas P.G.) or a geologist's representative.\n   112→\n   113→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d291c25-51de-4c78-8a82-6fde89edc795","uuid":"88e8bdef-79c3-46d6-aea2-b8c3e9ae21b3","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-19/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 19 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: diagram — A legend explaining the symbols and line types used in the site plan diagram for various utilities.\n\n### Legend\n| Symbol | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| W | Existing Water Line |\n| W | Proposed Water Line |\n| [Solid Square Symbol] | Single Water Service |\n| [Split Square Symbol] | Double Water Service |\n| WW | Existing Wastewater Line |\n| WW | Proposed Wastewater Line |\n| [Dashed Line] | Wastewater Service |\n| RW | Existing Reclaimed Water Line |\n| RW | Proposed Reclaimed Water Line |\n| E | Proposed Underground Electric |\n| SD | Proposed Storm Drain |\n| [FH Symbol] | Fire Hydrant |\n| [Valve Symbol] | Gate Valve |\n| [AARV Symbol] | AARV |\n| [T Symbol] | Transformer Pad |\n\n## Block 2: drawing — The main diagram showing the layout of the townhomes, including buildings, units, utility lines (water, wastewater, electric, storm drain), fire hydrants, cleanouts, and other site features. It includes numerous annotations and specific measurements for the utility infrastructure.\n\n[Detailed diagram showing the layout of multiple buildings labeled Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8, each containing multiple units. The diagram includes extensive utility routing with lines for water (W), wastewater (WW), and storm drain (SD). There are numerous callouts for specific components like '6\" Cleanout Traffic Bearing', 'Air Spade Trench', 'Duplex Grinder Pump Lift Station', 'Fire Demand Meter', and 'PVC Wastewater Service Line'. Topographic lines and elevations are also visible. Various easements and setbacks are marked. The layout is bounded by 'Valley View Road' to the west.]\n\n## Block 3: notes — The legal description of the property.\n\n### Legal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 4: notes — A note detailing the requirements for restraining joints on water line components.\n\n### Restrained Joints for all Water Line Bends, Tees, Gate Valves & Ends\nAll horizontal and vertical water line bends, tee's, gate valves and dead end's shall be restrained to the water main using factory restrained joint pipe as approved in SPL WW-27F, or, mechanical joint restraint devices as approved in SPL WW-27A.\n\n## Block 5: notes — A disclaimer regarding the accuracy of existing utility locations shown on the plans, requiring the contractor to field verify.\n\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n\n## Block 6: notes — A brief note directing the reader to the landscape plan sheet for the tree list.\n\n### Tree List\nRefer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n\n## Block 7: notes — A table listing the Drainage Fixture Units (DFU's) for each building, broken down by building type (e.g., 4Plex, 5Plex, 6Plex).\n\n### Drainage Fixture Unit Table\n| Building # | Building Type | DFU's |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | 4Plex | 136 |\n| 2 | 6Plex | 204 |\n| 3 | 5Plex | 170 |\n| 4 | 5Plex | 170 |\n| 5 | 4Plex | 136 |\n| 6 | 4Plex | 136 |\n| 7 | 5Plex | 170 |\n| 8 | 6Plex | 204 |\n\n## Block 8: notes — A note indicating the requirement for a pressure reducing valve for lots/structures with high water pressure.\n\n### PRV Note\nLots/Structure with 65 psi or greater require a pressure reducing valve, set at 65 psi, to be installed on the property owner's side of the right of way.\n\n## Block 9: notes\n\n## Block 11: table — The logo and contact information for Bleyl Engineering, the firm responsible for the planning, design, and management of the project.\n\n### Bleyl Engineering\nPlanning • Design • Management\n7701 San Felipe Blvd., Suite 200, Austin TX 78729\nTexas Firm Registration No. F-678\nTel. 512-454-2400\nwww.bleylengineering.com\n\nAustin | Bryan | Conroe | Houston\n\n## Block 12: table — A note regarding the requirements for private fire hydrants, including compliance with the Austin Fire Code and annual maintenance.\n\n### City of Austin - Water and Wastewater Utility - Special Services Division\n(512) 972-1060\nThis project has private hydrants located within the property. The property owner is required to comply with Austin Fire Code. Failure to comply may result in civil and/or criminal remedies available to the City. The performance of this obligation shall always rest with the owner or record. Fire hydrants on private property are required to serviced, maintained and flowed annually, using a contractor registered with the City to provide this service. This project includes __1__ private hydrants.\n\n## Block 13: text_block\n\n## Block 14: text_block — A disclaimer regarding the verification of data and calculations, stating the engineer's responsibility.\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n\n## Block 16: text_block — Information about the benchmarks used for the project, including elevation and location details.\n\n### Benchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 19: text_block — A list of utility notes instructing the contractor to verify existing utility locations, notify the engineer of conflicts, and specifying pipe materials for private domestic water and wastewater branches.\n\n### Utility Notes\n1. Contractor shall verify horizontal and vertical location of existing utilities prior to construction.\n2. Contractor shall verify location of existing line, point of connection and notify engineer of any conflicts/discrepancies prior to installation of improvements.\n3. All Private Domestic Water Branches to the buildings are to be 2\" HDPE service tubing. See Detail 520-AW-01B\n4. All Private Wastewater Branches to the buildings are to be 6\" PVC @ 2.0% min. See Detail 520-AW-01C\n\n## Block 20: text_block — A general note stating that the project is subject to the Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule and requires inspection by a geologist for trenches deeper than 5 feet.\n\n### Note\nThis project is subject to the Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule (COA ECM 1.12.0 and COA Item No. 658S of the SSM) provision that all trenching greater than 5 feet deep must be inspected by a geologist (Texas P.G.) or a geologist's representative.\n\n","numLines":113,"startLine":1,"totalLines":113}}}
