{"level":30,"time":1777033032266,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01MypuPECKi4BrG5iZKzpCMw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good information. The existing survey shows:\n- Overhead utility lines but no voltage labels (so cannot determine if distribution or transmission)\n- A guy wire with anchor shown\n- 5' electric easement and 5' public utility easement\n\nSince no voltage is labeled, I need to assess based on what's visible: these appear to be standard distribution lines based on the pole spacing and configuration, not major transmission lines (which would be taller structures with more spacing).\n\nNow let me finalize my analysis and prepare my findings output.\n\nBased on all my research, here are the checklist items I need to evaluate:\n\n### APPLICABLE AND FAILING/NOT-VERIFIABLE ITEMS:\n\n**EL-1.1** - Buildings don't maintain 7.5-foot clearance from overhead conductors\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE\n- Evidence: Overhead utility lines shown on Sheet 4 with poles near the northern and southern boundaries. Buildings 1-8 are positioned throughout the site. However, no surveyed conductor elevations or clearance dimensions from the outside conductor to building faces are provided on the site plan or building elevation sheets. The site plan shows horizontal distances from property lines/easement boundaries, but not from the actual conductor location. Without surveyed conductor locations and building clearance dimensions from the conductor, the requirement cannot be verified.\n\n**EL-1.2** - Customer facilities don't maintain 15-foot radial clearance from distribution conductors\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE\n- Evidence: The electrical design plan (Sheet 21) shows proposed underground electric service, not the existing overhead distribution lines. The site plan (Sheet 9) shows buildings and features but no radial clearance dimensions from overhead conductors. Without surveyed conductor locations, radial clearances cannot be verified.\n\n**EL-1.6** - Buildings adjacent to ROW don't maintain clearances\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE\n- Evidence: Valley View Road is adjacent on the western boundary. Sheet 4 shows overhead utility lines in the ROW. \n\nBuildings 1 and 2 are positioned near the western property line per Sheet 9, but no clearance dimensions from the conductor to these structures are documented.\n\n**EL-1.7** - Surveyed elevations and conductor locations not on building elevation sheets\n- Status: FAIL\n- Evidence: No building elevation sheets are included in the 39-sheet plan set (reviewed against the sheet index in the README). Without elevation sheets, surveyed conductor elevations and clearance annotations cannot be provided.\n\n**EL-1.8** - Section/plan exhibits don't show dimensions from conductor to buildings\n- Status: FAIL\n- Evidence: Sheet 9 (Site Plan) shows buildings and site features but lacks dimension lines indicating clearances from conductors to structures.\n\nSheet 11 (Grading Plan) identifies driveway locations and finished grades, yet provides no vertical clearance dimensions between the overhead lines and driveway surface. The existing utility line crosses near the property, but without surveyed conductor elevation paired with driveway grade data and clearance annotations, verification isn't possible.\n\n**EL-1.23** - Clearance dimensions measured from pole instead of outside conductor\n- Status: FAIL\n- Evidence: The plans show easement boundaries and property line dimensions, but dimension lines don't originate from the outside conductor location.\n\n**EL-1.27** - Facilities under/over distribution facilities or within easement\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE\n- Evidence: Sheet 4 shows overhead distribution lines and electric easements, with buildings positioned at least 25 feet from property lines containing these lines. However, precise surveyed conductor locations and confirmation that structures avoid the conductor routing are absent. Buildings appear adequately set back, but conductor survey data is needed for definitive verification.\n\n**EL-1.31** - Survey by registered surveyor not provided\n- Status: FAIL\n- Evidence: No surveyed conductor elevations or mid-span clearance data appear on any sheet. The Existing Conditions Survey on Sheet 4 shows surveyed pole locations but lacks conductor elevation information. Sheets 9 and 21 show building footprints and site layout without surveyed conductor positioning or elevation data required for clearance verification.\n\n**EL-1.46** - 48-hour notification note required\n- Status: PASS\n- Evidence: Sheet 3 General Notes includes \"Electric Transmission Notes\" Note 3 requiring a pre-construction safety meeting with Austin Energy 48 hours before construction, with contact information for Andrew Perez. Though the note references transmission lines while the code citation addresses distribution lines, the pre-construction coordination requirement for existing overhead lines is satisfied.\n\nHowever, the code citation should reference Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003 rather than UCM § 1.10.2.\n\nThe 48-hour notification requirement appears on Sheet 3 for transmission lines specifically. Since the checklist item requires notification before work near high-voltage overhead lines and the regulatory framework applies this to both distribution and transmission, I need to verify whether the current note adequately covers distribution lines or if additional language is needed.\n\nThe existing conditions survey marks overhead utility lines as \"O.H.U.L.\" without voltage designation, suggesting distribution lines may be present. However, the pre-construction coordination note with Austin Energy does address the core requirement regardless of line type. I'll consider this a pass and move forward with identifying remaining items.\n\nLooking at what else might apply: building projections would need elevation sheets I don't have, gas pump canopies aren't proposed, and the decomposed granite walkway described as elevated warrants checking. Various fence, wall, meter, and pole requirements could be relevant depending on site features shown. I need to review the plan sheets more carefully to determine which items are actually triggered by the proposed work.\n\nThe site includes 8 buildings, internal driveways, parking areas, sidewalks, retaining walls visible on Sheet 38, and landscaping with trees. Three transformer pads appear on Sheet 21 but are underground. The landscape plan on Sheets 30-31 is critical since I need to verify whether any trees fall within 10 feet of overhead conductors, and the retaining walls on Sheet 38 need clearance verification from those same conductors. I should systematically work through the remaining checklist items to identify what actually requires evaluation based on these proposed features.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1568,"cache_read_input_tokens":66854,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1568,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6f95fbc-fee7-4452-ac99-d9c79c3e8cef","uuid":"78696167-b587-4774-85b3-9a2564e66807"}
