{"level":30,"time":1777033293853,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"1.json","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01ANFUvBtv7GJnvy39VdiMwG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze this consolidated findings grouping for electrical utility clearances and compliance. This is clearly a **Utility (Electrical)** discipline review, with the primary concern being overhead electric conductor clearances, building proximity, vertical clearances, and code compliance with Austin Energy utility code (UCM § 1.10.x).\n\n## STEP 1: DISCIPLINE SCOPE\n\n**Discipline:** Utility (Electrical)  \n**Primary Concern:** Overhead electric line clearances (horizontal and vertical), building/structure/infrastructure proximity to conductors, conductor elevation documentation, and electrical safety compliance.\n\nAll findings in this grouping are **Tier 1 — Within Scope**. This is squarely an electrical utility discipline review. Some cross-department implications exist (e.g., landscaping trees, construction coordination), but the core deficiencies are all electrical utility compliance.\n\n## STEP 2: PROGRAM & OVERLAY HIERARCHY\n\nThe governing standard is the **Austin Code of Ordinances, Chapter 25-8, Utilities Code** (UCM), specifically:\n- UCM § 1.10.3 — Overhead conductor clearances (horizontal and vertical)\n- UCM § 1.10.2 — High-voltage work notifications\n- UCM § 1.10.6.2 — Driveway vertical clearances\n- UCM § 1.10.10.4 — Tree planting in utility easements\n- ECM § 2.4.1.D, ECM § 2.4.2.C — Utility-compatible landscape species\n\nThese are the governing programs. Some findings also reference \"Reviewer Convention\" or generic conventions, but the UCM and ECM sections are the actual code citations.\n\n## STEP 3: CONSOLIDATION\n\nCounting input findings:\n1. EL-1.1 (3 runs) — Building horizontal clearances to overhead lines\n2. EL-1.2 (3 runs) — Transformer pad radial clearances\n\n\n3. EL-1.7 (3 runs) — Elevation sheets lack conductor elevation data needed to verify clearances. EL-1.8 (3 runs) — Section and profile drawings missing clearance dimension callouts. EL-1.23 (3 runs) — Clearance measurements referenced to incorrect datum (pole centerline instead of conductor position). EL-1.37 (3 runs) — Vegetation near overhead lines fails utility-species compatibility requirements. EL-1.3 (2 runs) — Building projections (eaves, overhangs) not dimensioned relative to conductor position. EL-1.6 (2 runs) — Buildings positioned near Valley View Road ROW without utility clearance confirmation.\n\nAdditional issues identified with fewer occurrences: driveway clearances under overhead lines not verified (EL-1.18, 2 runs); facilities placed within electric easement boundaries or directly beneath conductors (EL-1.27, 2 runs); mid-span clearance data from registered surveyor not provided (EL-1.31, 2 runs); 48-hour utility notification requirement absent from construction documents (EL-1.46, 2 runs). Single-occurrence items include fence post clearances from poles (EL-1.10), retaining wall setbacks from conductors (EL-1.14), utility pole on northern property line not addressed in design (EL-1.22), sidewalk clearance from poles (EL-1.25), and EL-1.40.\n\nDown guy wires and anchor locations are not shown on plans (EL-1.42, 1 run), and down guy anchor easements lack proper depiction (EL-1.42, 1 run). Construction staging areas and equipment placement clearances from overhead lines need verification (EL-1.45, 1 run).\n\nAcross all 19 findings, the core issues center on missing surveyed conductor elevation data and clearance documentation, with multiple standards citations pointing to the same underlying gaps in utility coordination and plan completeness. requirements — 48-hour notifications and staging area clearances. Construction phase documentation needs to address both advance notice protocols and workspace management around utility infrastructure.\n\nLooking at the consolidated structure, five major comment categories emerge: building clearance documentation gaps, surveyed conductor data for site features, landscaping compatibility issues, pole and guy wire infrastructure documentation, and construction safety protocols. But Theme 1 is substantial enough to warrant refinement. Separating conductor elevation and horizontal clearance requirements for buildings from the broader surveyed data needs creates better specificity — the building-to-conductor relationship (both vertical and horizontal dimensions) forms one distinct documentation gap, while transformer, driveway, and other structural clearances constitute a separate concern.\n\nThis yields a clearer breakdown: conductor elevations and horizontal clearances relative to buildings; vertical clearance documentation for driveways and transformer facilities; landscaping restrictions; pole infrastructure and guy wire clearances; and construction notifications with staging requirements. The prohibition on facilities under conductors relates closely to the building clearance theme but addresses a distinct compliance question — whether existing structures violate absolute placement restrictions rather than measurement documentation. Folding this into the primary building comment with specific callout preserves the logical grouping while maintaining clarity on the compliance distinction. EL-1.1, EL-1.3, EL-1.6, EL-1.7, EL-1.8, EL-1.23, EL-1.27, EL-1.31\n\n**Headline:** \"Missing surveyed conductor elevations and clearance dimensions\"\n\n**Summary:** Overhead electric lines are present on and adjacent to the site. Buildings and site structures lack surveyed conductor elevation data and dimensioned clearances required to verify horizontal (7.5-foot sky-to-ground, 15-foot radial) and vertical clearances per UCM § 1.10.3. Building elevation sheets and section/profile exhibits with conductor measurements are absent. Additionally, code prohibits installing customer facilities directly under or over distribution conductors — this cannot be verified without surveyed conductor routing.\n\n**Details:**\n\n1. **EL-1.1** (3 runs, confidence 3): Buildings proposed near overhead lines on property boundaries. No surveyed conductor elevations or clearance dimensions shown on building elevations or plans. Cannot verify 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance. Citation: UCM § 1.10.3\n\n2. **EL-1.3** (2 runs, confidence 2): Building projections (overhangs, eaves, balconies, roofs, patios) not shown on elevation sheets relative to overhead conductors. Required 7.5-foot sky-to-ground and 15-foot radial clearances from projections cannot be assessed. Citation: UCM § 1.10.3\n\n3. **EL-1.6** (2 runs, confidence 2): Buildings 1 and 2 positioned within clearance distance of overhead utilities on Valley View Road right-of-way and northern boundary. Surveyed conductor elevations needed to confirm adequate separation and verify no customer facilities are located directly beneath distribution lines.\n\n4. **EL-1.7** (3 runs, confidence 3): Elevation drawings with surveyed conductor positions and clearance callouts to building faces and projections are missing from the plan set. Engineered, sealed documentation demonstrating vertical clearances must be provided.\n\n5. **EL-1.8** (3 runs, confidence 3): Clearance dimensions in sections and plans must originate from the outer conductor edge to building perimeters and projections, not from pole or multi-conductor centerlines. These annotated dimension lines are absent from submitted materials.\n\n6.\n\n**EL-1.23** (3 runs, confidence 3): Plan sheets contain no clearance dimensions. Since outermost conductors on multi-conductor runs can sit several feet from pole center, all dimensions must originate from the actual conductor location, not centerline references. This dimensioning approach is not documented.\n\n7. **EL-1.27** (2 runs, confidence 2): Code prohibits placing customer structures (buildings, driveways, facilities) beneath or above Austin Energy distribution lines regardless of clearance distances. The plans show buildings and driveways positioned near or potentially under overhead conductors without explicit confirmation that facilities avoid direct placement beneath or over the lines.\n\n8. **EL-1.31** (2 runs, confidence 2): A registered surveyor's mid-span conductor clearance survey or site-specific vertical analysis is absent. While building finished floor (686.25–685 ft MSL) and roof elevations are documented, overhead conductor elevations remain unknown, making clearance verification impossible and suggesting marginal conditions.\n\n**Sheets\n\n:** 4, 9, 21, 31  \n**Applicable Area:** Site-wide (all buildings and overhead facilities)  \n**Severity:** 3 (High — critical documentation gap blocking approval, safety concern)  \n**Confidence:** 3 (all findings flagged across all or majority of runs, core deficiency)  \n**CrossDep:** \"Coordination with Austin Energy required to provide registered surveyor mid-span clearance measurements and conductor routing; may necessitate conductor relocation or building repositioning.\"  \n**Attn:** \"Verify whether overhead electric line on northern boundary (shown on Sheet 4 existing survey) is intended to be removed, relocated, or left in place. Current site plan (Sheet 9) omits this pole location entirely, creating ambiguity about design intent.\"\n\n---\n\n### Comment 2: Missing Vertical Clearance Verification for Driveways and Transformer Pad\n\n**Applicable findings:** EL-1.2, EL-1.18\n\n**Headline:** \"Missing vertical clearance documentation for driveways and transformer\"\n\n**Summary:** Driveways crossing under overhead electric lines and a transformer pad positioned near distribution lines lack vertical clearance dimensions from the lowest conductor point (including attachments) to finished driveway/parking grades or transformer pad. Surveyed mid-span clearance heights and truck-traffic applicability determinations are missing, preventing verification of 15-foot (transformer radial) or 16/18-foot (driveway vertical) minimums.\n\n**Details:**\n\n1. **EL-1.2** (3 runs, confidence 3): Transformer pad shown near overhead distribution lines on property boundaries without clearance dimensions from outside conductor. Required 15-foot radial clearance in all directions cannot be verified. Citation: UCM § 1.10.3\n\n2. **EL-1.18** (2 runs, confidence 2): Internal driveways and parking areas proposed crossing under overhead electric line on southern boundary lack vertical clearance dimensions from lowest conductor to finished grades. Truck-traffic determination (16-foot vs. 18-foot minimum) not made. Registered surveyor mid-span clearance survey not provided. Citation: UCM § 1.10.6.2\n\n**Sheets:** 4, 9, 11, 21  \n**Applicable Area:** Site Interior (driveways, parking areas, transformer pad location)  \n**Severity:** 3 (High — vertical clearance violation risk, safety concern)  \n**Confidence:** 3 (both findings present across majority of runs, clear deficiency)  \n**CrossDep:** \"Coordination with Austin Energy required for registered surveyor mid-span clearance measurements; may require driveway realignment or transformer relocation to achieve compliance.\"  \n**Attn:** \"Confirm whether internal driveways will support truck traffic (refuse collection, emergency vehicles, deliveries), as this determines whether 16-foot or 18-foot clearance applies.\"\n\n---\n\n### Comment 3: Non-Utility-Compatible Landscaping Near Overhead Conductors\n\n**Applicable findings:** EL-1.37\n\n**Headline:** \"Proposed trees lack utility-compatible species designation\"\n\n**Summary:** Mitigation trees planted 10–15 feet from overhead distribution conductor on southern boundary must meet Utility Compatible requirements per ECM Appendix F (typically ≤20 feet mature height, marked UC). Proposed species not confirmed on landscape plan.\n\nThe landscape plan identifies Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Mexican Redbud, and Texas Mountain Laurel on Sheet 35, but cannot cross-reference which species go where on Sheet 31. Without this confirmation, I can't verify utility compatibility for trees within the critical 10-foot buffer zone. Non-compatible species will eventually grow into the overhead line, creating a safety hazard. ECM § 2.4.1.D and § 2.4.2.C require this verification; severity is 2.\n\nThe recommendation is to coordinate with the City Arborist and Austin Energy to finalize species selection and confirm utility-compatible designations. The applicant should provide a marked-up landscape plan with specific species identified at each location, cross-referenced to the plant schedule, along with ECM Appendix F utility-compatible verification for all trees within 10 lateral feet of overhead conductors.\n\n---\n\nUtility pole locations, guy wires, and associated clearances undocumented. The existing conditions survey shows utility poles on the northern and southern boundaries, but the proposed site plan doesn't indicate their status—whether they're being removed, relocated, or kept. There's no documentation of down guy wires, anchor locations, or clearance dimensions to proposed site features like fences, retaining walls, driveways, and sidewalks. Down guy anchor easements required for distribution poles aren't shown on easement exhibits, making it impossible to verify that pole-related conflicts are resolved or that adequate clearances are maintained.\n\nThe fences proposed near existing utility poles lack measured distances between fence posts and pole locations, so I can't confirm the 10-foot minimum horizontal clearance requirement is met.\n\nRetaining walls are detailed throughout the site perimeter near overhead electric facilities on the property boundaries, but there are no dimension lines or clearance annotations showing distances from the outside conductor to the wall faces. Without surveyor data and section exhibits, the horizontal sky-to-ground clearance cannot be verified.\n\nThe existing utility pole on the northern property boundary appears on the existing conditions survey but is omitted from the proposed site plan with no notation about relocation, removal, or retention. If the pole remains, site improvements within 30 feet would require relocating it to a minimum of 30 feet away, but the current plan doesn't address this conflict.\n\nA public sidewalk is proposed along the Valley View Road frontage near existing utility poles shown on the western property line in the right-of-way. The exact clearance distances from the sidewalk to each pole location are not dimensioned, making it impossible to verify the sidewalk setback from the poles. Existing survey documentation shows utility poles on both northern and southern boundaries, and while down guy wires and anchor locations are typically omitted from plans, they're critical infrastructure that could conflict with proposed site improvements like driveways, accessible paths, and buildings near the pole bases. This requires verification from Austin Energy and supporting survey documentation. The easement exhibit and site plan also need review to ensure any required down guy anchor easements—which typically require a dedicated 10-foot-wide easement extending at least 5 feet past the anchor ground entry point per Austin Energy standards—are properly depicted on the plat and easement exhibits if the poles have down guys installed.\n\nTo resolve this, I need to contact Austin Energy directly for registered surveyor documentation showing pole locations, guy wire routing, and anchor locations. I should also confirm whether the northern boundary pole on Sheet 4 is on-site or off-site and clarify the design intent regarding retention or relocation. Once the pole status is confirmed, all clearances need to be re-verified.\n\nAdditionally, the plans lack required construction safety notifications mandating that the owner and contractor notify Austin Energy at least 48 hours before beginning work near high-voltage overhead lines, as specified in Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752. There's also no construction logistics or staging plan documenting equipment laydown zones, work platforms, or crane operation areas to ensure adequate clearances. ) and general notes lack required notification language mandating that the owner and contractor notify Austin Energy at least 48 hours before commencing work near high-voltage overhead lines per Texas Health & Safety Code Chapter 752, Section 752.003. While Sheet 3 addresses pre-construction safety meetings for transmission facilities, there's no general contractor notification requirement documented for all overhead electric facilities—both distribution and transmission lines. This notification is mandatory whenever overhead electric facilities are present on or adjacent to the site. The deficiency spans multiple sheets and represents a substantive construction safety gap requiring documentation in plan notes and general conditions.\n\nCoordination with Safety and Construction Administration is needed to establish proper construction notification procedures and staging area logistics, potentially requiring Austin Energy and electrical contractor approval. The fix involves adding explicit language to either the cover sheet or Sheet 3 stating the 48-hour notification requirement, along with a construction staging plan that demonstrates equipment and work platforms maintain a 10-foot radial clearance from all energized overhead lines.\n\nThe area assignments break down into five categories: site-wide coverage for all buildings and overhead facilities, site interior for driveways and parking areas, site interior for the southern landscape boundary, site-wide for pole infrastructure at property edges, and plan documentation for administrative notes. These align with the fixed set of categories—Plan Documentation for administrative items, Site-wide for full-property scope, and Site Interior for on-site features.\n\nThe consolidation is working well: 19 input findings compressed into 5 output comments yields a 3.8:1 ratio, matching the target. Each finding maps to exactly one comment with no duplication, ensuring complete coverage without redundancy. I need to structure the JSON output to properly represent all the mapped findings with their corresponding comments and details.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":9,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31748,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":31748,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e4bead79-d9ad-4687-a843-03346abdd75e","uuid":"a1ff65bb-4129-4a0a-ae74-71e6fbbd1db2"}
{"level":30,"time":1777033295338,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"1.json","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01ANFUvBtv7GJnvy39VdiMwG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll synthesize these 19 consolidated electrical utility findings into a focused set of review comments. This grouping covers overhead electric conductor clearances, building proximity to utility infrastructure, landscaping compliance, and construction safety requirements under Austin Energy code (UCM § 1.10.x).\n\n**Discipline:** Utility (Electrical) — all findings are Tier 1 (within scope). The discipline owns overhead conductor clearances, building/structure setbacks from utilities, utility-compatible landscaping, and related infrastructure documentation.\n\n**Consolidation:** 19 input findings consolidate into **5 comments** (~3.8:1 ratio), organized by:\n1. Missing surveyed conductor elevations and clearance dimensions (buildings and structures)\n2. Missing vertical clearance verification (driveways, transformer)\n3. Non-utility-compatible landscaping\n4. Pole, guy wire, and associated infrastructure documentation gaps\n5. Construction safety notifications and staging clearances"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":9,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31748,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":31748,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e4bead79-d9ad-4687-a843-03346abdd75e","uuid":"c40c727d-815e-4ed0-9149-a6ff7dfea274"}
{"level":30,"time":1777033345378,"pid":122,"hostname":"7e1d7a35-fad","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"2.json","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01EDrcEiAj2WFRc8pbUygsCa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me carefully analyze this task. I'm being asked to synthesize a set of consolidated findings into a structured set of review comments. Let me break down what I'm given:\n\n1. **Grouping Data**: 11 findings (2:EL-2.1, 2:EL-2.3, 2:EL-2.6, 2:EL-2.7, 2:EL-2.10, 2:EL-2.14, 2:EL-2.15, 2:EL-2.8, 2:EL-2.9, 2:EL-2.11, 2:EL-2.12, 2:EL-2.13)\n2. **Total Runs**: 3\n3. **Primary Concern**: This is clearly about electrical utility compliance with landscape plans. All findings relate to Code UCM § 1.10.10.4 (and some \"Reviewer Convention\").\n\nLet me work through the steps:\n\n### STEP 1 — DISCIPLINE SCOPE\n\nThis is a **Utility (Electrical)** discipline review. The primary concern is:\n- Tree and landscaping clearance distances from overhead electrical lines, distribution conductors, utility poles, transformer pads, and underground electrical equipment\n- Species selection based on utility compatibility\n- Documentation and verification of compliance\n\nAll findings are **Tier 1 (Within Scope)** — they directly relate to electrical utility requirements that the utility department enforces. No cross-departmental issues here unless there's an arborist involved, but the directive is coming from Austin Energy/electrical utility.\n\n### STEP 2 — PROGRAM & OVERLAY HIERARCHY\n\nThe governing standard is **UCM § 1.10.10.4** (likely Austin Code Municipal Section on utility clearances). This is a citywide requirement, not a project-specific condition or optional program. All citations should reference this section.\n\n\n### STEP 3 — CONSOLIDATION\n\nLooking at the 11 findings, I can identify several distinct compliance issues: trees planted too close to overhead electrical lines with varying distances across multiple runs; large trees exceeding 40 feet at maturity positioned inadequately from OHE; trees within 8.5 feet of utility poles when 10 feet is required; trees at 1.7 feet from pad-mounted transformers when 10 feet is mandated; and unclear identification of utility infrastructure on the plans.\n\nThe documentation gaps are significant: species aren't named on the landscape plan, only shown as symbols; no dimension lines or clearance measurements appear on the drawings; trees in prohibited zones lack Austin Energy approval documentation; underground electrical equipment clearances of 5 feet aren't verified; root barriers and equipment locations aren't detailed; large versus smaller trees aren't differentiated; and proper measurement methodology with dimensioned clearances is absent from the plan.\n\nLooking at the source IDs, there are 12 distinct findings across the range 2:EL-2.1 through 2:EL-2.15 (with 2:EL-2.2, 2:EL-2.4, and 2:EL-2.5 not appearing). The natural groupings suggest consolidating into roughly 3 comments. The overhead distribution line issues cluster together—trees positioned too close to OHE conductors combined with incompatible species selection and oversized tree restrictions all stem from the same core problem of proximity and species compatibility. Utility infrastructure details form another group, covering unidentified utility poles and inadequate tree setbacks from them.\n\nUnderground equipment represents a separate concern where clearances from subsurface infrastructure haven't been verified and root barriers lack documentation in equipment zones. The plan documentation gaps—missing species labels, absent dimension lines with clearance notations, unlabeled large trees, and incomplete measurement methodology notes—constitute a distinct category focused on drawing clarity and completeness. Finally, the lack of Austin Energy approval for non-compliant placements could either stand alone or integrate with the overhead distribution line group depending on how the comments are structured. Energy approval requirements for overhead distribution lines—trees positioned too close with incompatible species and lacking Austin Energy compliance (EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.15). Utility poles and transformer pads need clearer location marking and adequate tree clearances (EL-2.6, EL-2.7). Underground electrical equipment requires verified clearances and documented root barriers (EL-2.8, EL-2.9). The plan documentation has multiple gaps: unlabeled species, missing dimensions, and unclear measurement methodology (EL-2.10 through EL-2.14). This consolidates 12 findings into 4 substantive comments, achieving meaningful grouping without losing specificity.\n\nThe first three comments address physical site violations requiring actual changes—relocating trees or selecting compliant species—while the fourth targets documentation deficiencies that prevent verification. These require different remediation approaches. All findings fall squarely within the electrical utility discipline's scope, with no Tier 3 items to exclude.\n\nFor the overhead distribution line clearances, the violations cluster around two core issues: non-utility-compatible species positioned too close to conductors, and unidentified vegetation preventing verification. Run 2 shows Cedar Elm and Live Oak trees 3.1-6 feet from the line when distribution standards require 15 feet lateral clearance; Run 1 presents the same species at 0-1.5 feet with mature heights exceeding 40 feet, demanding 25 feet clearance per the utility compatibility matrix. Run 3 compounds the problem by leaving tree species unidentified on the landscape plan, making compliance assessment impossible. The pattern repeats across multiple runs—incompatible species at insufficient distances, with some locations lacking species identification entirely.\n\nAdditionally, trees positioned 15-25 feet from distribution conductors lack required Austin Energy approval, and any variations from standard clearance zones need explicit authorization. The deficiency spans sheets 31 and 35 along the southern property boundary near Valley View Road, governed by UCM § 1.10.10.4. the facts directly. Based on the violation description, the trees are along the southern boundary near overhead lines. I'll use \"Southern Boundary\" as the applicable area since that's what's documented in the violation.\n\n**Applicable Area**: \"Southern Boundary\"\n\nNow I need to finalize the severity assessment. This is a high-severity issue because it involves safety compliance with electrical clearance requirements—a blocking issue that affects critical infrastructure. The trees' proximity to distribution lines creates a genuine hazard. Confidence is high since all three inspectors identified the same violation. izing variations to standard clearance distances requires explicit Austin Energy approval per UCM § 1.10.10.4. The submitted landscape plan lacks documented written authorization for any deviations from non-compatible species restrictions or reduced clearance requirements.\n\nI need to reconsider how to structure these findings. Each detail should consolidate information across runs using the runComments field rather than creating separate entries. For EL-2.1, the three runs (nv, f, nv) should be combined into a single detail with per-run commentary showing the specific violations in each run. Similarly, EL-2.3 needs to capture the height threshold exceedances and lateral clearance shortfalls across the different measurement approaches, and EL-2.15 should document the missing Austin Energy approval as a unified finding with run-specific notes about where documentation is absent.\n\nThe detail text should articulate the primary deficiency that emerges across runs—trees positioned too close to overhead electrical infrastructure—while runComments provide the granular measurements and species identifications from each individual run. For EL-2.3, the core issue is large trees exceeding mature heights at insufficient lateral distances, with runComments showing the specific heights measured (Cedar Elm 90 ft, Live Oak 60 ft) and the required 25-foot clearance. For EL-2.15, the central finding is the absence of documented Austin Energy approval for non-compliant tree placements, with runComments indicating which runs identified this gap.\n\nThe Comment 1 structure captures the headline as a violation of clearance distances, the summary synthesizing the Cedar Elm and Live Oak positioning (0-6 feet from conductor) against UCM § 1.10.10.4 requirements (15 feet minimum for distribution, 25 feet for trees over 40 feet), and notes the missing approval documentation. The details array then breaks down the specific clearance violation with citation and source reference, while runComments document what each run verified—Run 1 noting the 0-1.5 foot proximity and inability to conclusively determine utility-compatibility from submitted plans, Run 2 confirming the 3.1-6 foot range with Cedar Elm and Live Oak identified as non-utility-compatible, and Run 3 unable to verify due to unidentified species.\n\nThe second detail object addresses the height-based clearance requirement, noting that both species exceed the 40-foot threshold (Cedar Elm at 90 feet, Live Oak at 60 feet) and therefore require 25 feet lateral clearance rather than 15 feet, creating violations of 19-25 feet based on the 0-6 foot measured distances. UCM § 1.10.10.4. Trees must be repositioned or replaced with trees of compliant mature height (less than 40 feet).\n\nFor the third review run, species identification cannot be confirmed at specific planting locations since the landscape plan lacks this notation. Live Oak and Cedar Elm are listed in the plant schedule but their mature heights per ECM Appendix F cannot be cross-referenced to actual site locations. The plan needs to clearly identify species and mature height for each tree to verify compliance with the 25-foot clearance requirement.\n\nThe plans place Cedar Elm and Live Oak within restricted clearance zones—15-25 feet from overhead distribution conductors and 10 feet from utility poles and transformer equipment. Austin Energy's written approval for these exceptions is not documented anywhere in the submitted materials, site plan notes, or plan set. Per UCM § 1.10.10.4, any variations from standard clearance requirements require explicit written authorization from Austin Energy, which must be provided. distances of 0-6 feet represent 19-25 foot violations. Cedar Elm and Live Oak mature heights (90 ft and 60 ft respectively) exceed the 40-foot threshold, triggering the 25-foot lateral clearance requirement per UCM § 1.10.10.4.\n\nFor utility infrastructure concerns, I'm identifying issues with pole proximity and transformer clearances. Trees are positioned too close to utility poles—one measured at 8.5 feet when 10 feet minimum is required—and poles lack clear identification on the landscape plan. Transformer clearances also need verification against code standards. I need to complete the partial thought first. Looking at the next thinking, it continues: \"Southern property boundary; central parking area\" but appears cut off. Let me finish this thought by copying to the next logical break.\n\nThe applicable areas are the southern property boundary where utility poles are located and the central parking area with the landscape island adjacent to the electrical transformer pad. These specific locations make the clearance violations particularly problematic since they're in high-visibility areas of the site.\n\nFor the sheets, the electrical plan (21) and landscape plan (31) both document these issues. The severity warrants a medium rating given that plan revisions are necessary to address the clearance violations and improve identification of utility infrastructure. Confidence in these findings is solid based on the multiple plan reviews showing consistent documentation of the violations across both electrical and landscape drawings. plans, preventing verification of all tree-to-pole distances. Another issue involves a proposed tree positioned 1.7 lateral feet from a pad-mounted transformer access door, also violating the 10-foot minimum clearance requirement under UCM § 1.10.10.4. The transformer pad locations are identified on the electrical plan but lack cross-reference on the landscape plan, making it difficult to assess compliance across both documents.\n\nUnderground electrical equipment presents additional verification challenges. Conduits and pull boxes shown on the electrical plan don't appear on the landscape plan, and trees must maintain 5-foot clearance from this underground infrastructure. Furthermore, trees within 20 feet of underground equipment require 4-foot deep root barriers positioned 5 feet away, but the landscape plan lacks the necessary detail to confirm these requirements are met. foot clearance and 20-foot zones with root barriers cannot be verified.\n\nThe applicable area falls under Utilities & Infrastructure since it concerns equipment shown across plan sheets. This issue appears on sheets 21 and 31, with medium severity since compliance cannot be confirmed without clearer plan documentation. Confidence is low—only one of three analysis runs identified this concern. The core problem is that underground electrical equipment locations lack sufficient clarity and dimensioning to verify the required clearances and root barrier zones for proposed trees. shown on electrical plan (Sheet 21) are not marked on landscape plan (Sheet 31). Trees must maintain 5-foot minimum lateral clearance from underground electrical equipment per UCM § 1.10.10.4. Provide location overlay showing underground equipment and adjacent tree clearances.\n\nUnderground equipment locations aren't detailed on the landscape plan. Trees within 20 feet of underground electrical equipment need 4-foot deep root barriers positioned 5 feet from the equipment per UCM § 1.10.\n\n10.4. I need a detailed location plan that identifies equipment zones and specifies root barrier requirements to verify which trees fall within the affected areas.\n\nThe landscape plan is missing critical documentation across multiple standards. Trees lack species identification, size differentiation isn't clear, and clearance dimensions are absent—both the measurements themselves and the reference points they're measured from. There are no dimension lines or notations showing clearances. All of this prevents verification that the design meets code requirements. The plant schedule on Sheet 35 lacks connection to the actual tree locations shown on Sheet 31, making it impossible to determine which species are planted where. Additionally, clearance measurements don't specify the proper reference points—specifically measuring from tree trunk center to the outside edge of overhead conductors.\n\nFor documentation purposes, this falls under \"Plan Documentation.\" The relevant sheets are 31 (landscape plan), 35 (plant schedule), and 21 (electrical plan for utility reference). This is a medium-severity issue since it prevents verification of code compliance. Looking at the pattern of occurrences, EL-2.10 appears twice across different runs, while EL-2.11 shows up once.\n\nThe remaining findings—EL-2.12, EL-2.13, and EL-2.14—follow a similar pattern, with most appearing in single runs except EL-2.14 which shows up twice. Given this inconsistency across the review cycles, I'm assigning a confidence level of 2. The key concern is that several findings only surfaced in one run, so I need to flag that all trees in utility clearance zones require proper labeling and dimensioning verification on the landscape plan. For the first detail, the landscape plan currently identifies trees only through regulatory category codes rather than actual species designations. all trees within 50 feet of overhead electrical facilities. The plant schedule lists four species—Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Mexican Redbud, and Texas Mountain Laurel—but these aren't tied to their locations on the plan, making it difficult to identify which trees correspond to which symbols.\n\nThe landscape plan fails to differentiate between mature tree sizes, which is critical for electrical clearance. Cedar Elm reaches 90 feet at maturity while Mexican Redbud only grows to 20 feet, yet both are shown with identical symbols. Mature heights need to be clearly indicated so clearance requirements can be properly verified.\n\nThe overhead electrical lines and tree symbols appear on the landscape plan but lack dimension lines, clearance distances, or measurement notes connecting them. Cross-referencing with the electrical plan and adding specific dimensions showing clear distances from tree trunk centers to outside conductors is needed to verify compliance with the 15-foot distribution and 50-foot transmission line minimums. tree 21-trunk 22-centers 23-to 24-outside 25-conductors 26-per 27-UCM 28-§ 29-1.10.10.4 30-measurement 31-methodology 32-(15-foot 33-distribution 34-50-foot 35-transmission 36-minimums). The plan lacks dimensions and measurement methodology specifications for tree-to-conductor clearances. I need to establish that clearances must be measured from the planting location symbol to the outside conductor, then add dimension callouts and reference notes on the landscape plan for all trees situated within utility clearance zones. § 1.10.10.4, not the projected canopy edge. Dimension lines and notes should confirm the measurement methodology originates from trunk center to the outside conductor perimeter.\n\nLooking at these two details, they're addressing nearly identical issues—both flagging missing dimensioned clearances and requiring measurements from tree trunk center per code. The distinction between them seems minimal, with EL-2.12 focusing on all clearance measurements originating from trunk center and EL-2.13 emphasizing the measurement methodology itself. They could potentially be consolidated into a single detail rather than maintained as separate findings. These findings both address missing dimensions and cite the same regulatory section (UCM § 1.10.10.4), so they should merge into a single detail. The challenge is that the schema only allows one sourceId per detail, but the instruction requires accounting for every input finding with its sourceId visible. I need to check whether listing both sourceIds in the runComments would satisfy the traceability requirement, or if I should keep them separate despite the content overlap. So each detail can only reference a single finding via `sourceId`. That means I can't combine findings into one detail.\n\nI have five related findings about documentation on the landscape plan, but EL-2.12 and EL-2.13 aren't actually duplicates—one addresses the general lack of measurement methodology while the other specifically calls out the missing reference point for tree measurements. I should keep them as separate details within the same comment. This gives me five details across four total comments, which hits roughly the 3:1 consolidation ratio I'm aiming for.\n\nNow I need to structure the JSON output properly. Each comment requires a headline, summary, sheets array, applicableArea, severity, and confidence level. Let me verify I have all 12 findings accounted for and map them correctly to their respective comments.\n\nFor the first comment on overhead distribution lines, I'm calculating confidence based on how many runs flagged each finding—EL-2.1 and EL-2.3 both triggered across multiple runs, while EL-2.15 needs to be included in this grouping as well.\n\nFor the second comment about poles and transformers, both EL-2.6 and EL-2.7 appear in 2 of the 3 runs, giving them each a confidence of 2, which also sets the overall comment confidence at 2.\n\nThe third comment on underground equipment starts with EL-2... (because some have 2 runs). Detail confidence = 